{"parentUuid":"7ac93137-39ac-43d9-9524-3c028b02f5df","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4mb3HZhZxa2HhbweJX5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LTBdhYq518hDSfKc9G29hS","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://github.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books","prompt":"What is this repository? Summarize its purpose, structure (directories/files), the format and style of the rules/books it contains, any stated methodology or principles for writing agent rules, and notable examples. List the file/folder names."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":673,"cache_read_input_tokens":367553,"output_tokens":157,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":673,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":157,"cache_read_input_tokens":367553,"cache_creation_input_tokens":673,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":673},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":906,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LTBdhYq518hDSfKc9G29hS":{"url":"https://github.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books","prompt":"What is this repository? Summarize its purpose, structure (directories/files), the format and style of the rules/books it contains, any stated methodology or principles for writing agent rules, and notable examples. List the file/folder names."}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch4mb2zhYTjfvzgY7XM6o","requestRef":"07759c4d-e4e3-43a3-9cd3-2cfdc43a2a01","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"7ea6b1b1-70e5-4866-b4e6-7c8b382398b2","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:10:50.961Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"7ea6b1b1-70e5-4866-b4e6-7c8b382398b2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a64db6bd-a503-4549-bc43-76dbb357cf44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LTBdhYq518hDSfKc9G29hS","type":"tool_result","content":"## Overview\n\nThis is a fork of `ciembor/agent-rules-books` (v0.5, MIT licensed, about 595 stars). It provides ready-to-use `AGENTS.md`-style rule sets for AI coding agents such as Codex, Cursor, and Claude Code. Each set is distilled from a classic software engineering book, covering design, architecture, refactoring, legacy code, reliability, and data systems.\n\n## Structure\n\n**Top level**\n- `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `.gitignore`, `books-ai-rules.png`\n- `docs/`: `USAGE.md`, `COMPATIBILITY.md`, `CRITICISM.md`, `ADDING_THE_BOOK.md`\n- `_rule-workbench/`\n\n**Rule set folders (15 books)**\n- `a-philosophy-of-software-design`\n- `clean-architecture`\n- `clean-code`\n- `code-complete`\n- `designing-data-intensive-applications`\n- `domain-driven-design`\n- `domain-driven-design-distilled`\n- `implementing-domain-driven-design`\n- `patterns-of-enterprise-application-architecture`\n- `refactoring`\n- `refactoring-guru`\n- `release-it`\n- `the-pragmatic-programmer`\n- `working-effectively-with-legacy-code`\n\nEach folder contains three Markdown files: `<name>.md` (full), `<name>.mini.md`, and `<name>.nano.md`.\n\n## Format and Style\n\n- **Three tiers per book:**\n  - `mini`: recommended for most real tasks (roughly 28–47 rules).\n  - `nano`: compact fallback for tight context budgets (roughly 14–26 rules).\n  - `full`: canonical, complete reference (roughly 177–523 rules).\n- **Tool-agnostic Markdown**, usable as `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, Cursor rules, Copilot instructions, or skills.\n- The README reports line, rule, and byte counts for each file.\n\n## Stated Methodology\n\n- Rules are practical working agreements for agents, not summaries or study notes.\n- Rules intentionally avoid reproducing book text.\n- Concrete, enumerated rules are favored over simply naming a book. The author's experiment supports this.\n- Use cases are tied to each book, e.g., refactoring for APOSD, legacy work for Feathers' book.\n- Rule sets are meant to be combined, with a compatibility guide.\n- Scope is controlled through always-on versus on-demand loading and skills.\n\n## Notable Examples\n\n- **APOSD refactor experiment:** Using the `mini` rules, a refactor was rated about 74/100 by ChatGPT. A branch with only a single line naming the book scored about 46/100. The author calls this an early qualitative signal, not a benchmark.\n- **Reek smell check:** The difference was small (1083 smells originally versus 1077 after the mini refactor), suggesting the gain was mainly architectural rather than in raw smell counts.\n- **Refactoring.Guru set:** Designed to diagnose smells, choose a safe treatment, preserve behavior, and stop before cleanup becomes a redesign.\n- **Release It! set:** Targets failure handling, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, and observability for production services."}]},"uuid":"3457b4d8-8a86-420f-9153-676d5724a35a","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:01.061Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":361398,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Overview\n\nThis is a fork of `ciembor/agent-rules-books` (v0.5, MIT licensed, about 595 stars). It provides ready-to-use `AGENTS.md`-style rule sets for AI coding agents such as Codex, Cursor, and Claude Code. Each set is distilled from a classic software engineering book, covering design, architecture, refactoring, legacy code, reliability, and data systems.\n\n## Structure\n\n**Top level**\n- `README.md`, `LICENSE`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `.gitignore`, `books-ai-rules.png`\n- `docs/`: `USAGE.md`, `COMPATIBILITY.md`, `CRITICISM.md`, `ADDING_THE_BOOK.md`\n- `_rule-workbench/`\n\n**Rule set folders (15 books)**\n- `a-philosophy-of-software-design`\n- `clean-architecture`\n- `clean-code`\n- `code-complete`\n- `designing-data-intensive-applications`\n- `domain-driven-design`\n- `domain-driven-design-distilled`\n- `implementing-domain-driven-design`\n- `patterns-of-enterprise-application-architecture`\n- `refactoring`\n- `refactoring-guru`\n- `release-it`\n- `the-pragmatic-programmer`\n- `working-effectively-with-legacy-code`\n\nEach folder contains three Markdown files: `<name>.md` (full), `<name>.mini.md`, and `<name>.nano.md`.\n\n## Format and Style\n\n- **Three tiers per book:**\n  - `mini`: recommended for most real tasks (roughly 28–47 rules).\n  - `nano`: compact fallback for tight context budgets (roughly 14–26 rules).\n  - `full`: canonical, complete reference (roughly 177–523 rules).\n- **Tool-agnostic Markdown**, usable as `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, Cursor rules, Copilot instructions, or skills.\n- The README reports line, rule, and byte counts for each file.\n\n## Stated Methodology\n\n- Rules are practical working agreements for agents, not summaries or study notes.\n- Rules intentionally avoid reproducing book text.\n- Concrete, enumerated rules are favored over simply naming a book. The author's experiment supports this.\n- Use cases are tied to each book, e.g., refactoring for APOSD, legacy work for Feathers' book.\n- Rule sets are meant to be combined, with a compatibility guide.\n- Scope is controlled through always-on versus on-demand loading and skills.\n\n## Notable Examples\n\n- **APOSD refactor experiment:** Using the `mini` rules, a refactor was rated about 74/100 by ChatGPT. A branch with only a single line naming the book scored about 46/100. The author calls this an early qualitative signal, not a benchmark.\n- **Reek smell check:** The difference was small (1083 smells originally versus 1077 after the mini refactor), suggesting the gain was mainly architectural rather than in raw smell counts.\n- **Refactoring.Guru set:** Designed to diagnose smells, choose a safe treatment, preserve behavior, and stop before cleanup becomes a redesign.\n- **Release It! set:** Targets failure handling, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, and observability for production services.","durationMs":9995,"url":"https://github.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7ea6b1b1-70e5-4866-b4e6-7c8b382398b2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
