{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","prNumber":519,"prUrl":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/519","prRepository":"mikeholownych/nexus-code","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:12.565Z"}
{"parentUuid":"6ad02a3d-6a30-4ea2-9fa6-0b6fc24b1fdf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a64db6bd-a503-4549-bc43-76dbb357cf44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MyC5bMVARNTWgf8HPfXYzh","type":"tool_result","content":"The criticisms focus on how long rule files can backfire, and the author's responses mostly shift the project toward smaller, selectively loaded rule sets.\n\n**Context and token cost**\n- Large rule files can use up tokens and crowd out task-specific context. The author's response is a three-tier release model (`full`, `mini`, `nano`), with `mini` recommended for daily use and `full` kept as reference. Guidance now warns against loading everything globally.\n- The author also concedes that the remaining gap is user behavior: \"The remaining gap is behavioral rather than structural.\"\n\n**Instruction overload and ignored rules**\n- A critic notes that \"LLMs do not reliably obey hundreds of instructions at the same time.\" Competing instructions can create ambiguity.\n- The response is to compress toward decision-changing rules and triggers. The author admits there is no empirical data on how often models follow the compressed sets.\n\n**Overengineering**\n- Heavyweight architecture rules applied to small tasks can add \"unnecessary layers, abstractions, factories, ports, adapters, aggregates, or domain events.\"\n- The author's response is to stress task-scoped loading and \"use this only when warranted\" language. They acknowledge the limit: \"the repo can tell you not to load heavyweight rules for trivial tasks, but it cannot enforce good task selection.\"\n\n**Pseudo-compliance**\n- Rules may encourage code that looks principled while missing the actual task constraints, producing \"surface-level compliance instead of practical correctness.\"\n- The response favors operational rules and traceability over broad slogans, but there are still no outcome evals showing the agent solves the real task.\n\n**Conflicting sources**\n- Books such as Clean Code, DDD, and PoEAA can push toward different architectural tradeoffs.\n- The author recommends one primary always-on rule set with secondary sets loaded on demand. No formal conflict matrix exists yet.\n\n**Unmeasured effectiveness**\n- The critic says there is no \"clear measurement of improvement,\" with no benchmarks, defect counts, or review-effort data.\n- The author acknowledges that the measurability problem is largely unsolved, with only auditable compression in place.\n\n**Book-derived vs. incident-derived rules**\n- The most valuable rules may come from real failures, but the repository is \"still primarily book-derived, not incident-derived.\"\n- There is no formal mechanism yet for turning postmortems or failed agent runs into rules.\n\n**Model priors and outdated principles**\n- Models may already know these principles, so a full book-sized rule set may not be better than a short reminder. The author shifted to framing `mini` as \"a short, decision-changing reminder.\"\n- Some principles may be dated in the AI coding era. The author filters content through an agent-oriented process but acknowledges the source material remains rooted in classic books.\n\n**Source and licensing concerns**\n- A commenter raised concerns about the origin of the distilled content. The author reframed the files as \"practical engineering instructions inspired by the books, not official materials,\" and rated the issue only partly addressed.\n- The project will not seek formal legal clearance: it \"will not attempt to provide formal legal clearance or legal analysis.\" I can't assess the legal questions here."}]},"uuid":"d00d2d66-0e13-49ad-9524-f24ab08c9fe9","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:15.001Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":11604,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The criticisms focus on how long rule files can backfire, and the author's responses mostly shift the project toward smaller, selectively loaded rule sets.\n\n**Context and token cost**\n- Large rule files can use up tokens and crowd out task-specific context. The author's response is a three-tier release model (`full`, `mini`, `nano`), with `mini` recommended for daily use and `full` kept as reference. Guidance now warns against loading everything globally.\n- The author also concedes that the remaining gap is user behavior: \"The remaining gap is behavioral rather than structural.\"\n\n**Instruction overload and ignored rules**\n- A critic notes that \"LLMs do not reliably obey hundreds of instructions at the same time.\" Competing instructions can create ambiguity.\n- The response is to compress toward decision-changing rules and triggers. The author admits there is no empirical data on how often models follow the compressed sets.\n\n**Overengineering**\n- Heavyweight architecture rules applied to small tasks can add \"unnecessary layers, abstractions, factories, ports, adapters, aggregates, or domain events.\"\n- The author's response is to stress task-scoped loading and \"use this only when warranted\" language. They acknowledge the limit: \"the repo can tell you not to load heavyweight rules for trivial tasks, but it cannot enforce good task selection.\"\n\n**Pseudo-compliance**\n- Rules may encourage code that looks principled while missing the actual task constraints, producing \"surface-level compliance instead of practical correctness.\"\n- The response favors operational rules and traceability over broad slogans, but there are still no outcome evals showing the agent solves the real task.\n\n**Conflicting sources**\n- Books such as Clean Code, DDD, and PoEAA can push toward different architectural tradeoffs.\n- The author recommends one primary always-on rule set with secondary sets loaded on demand. No formal conflict matrix exists yet.\n\n**Unmeasured effectiveness**\n- The critic says there is no \"clear measurement of improvement,\" with no benchmarks, defect counts, or review-effort data.\n- The author acknowledges that the measurability problem is largely unsolved, with only auditable compression in place.\n\n**Book-derived vs. incident-derived rules**\n- The most valuable rules may come from real failures, but the repository is \"still primarily book-derived, not incident-derived.\"\n- There is no formal mechanism yet for turning postmortems or failed agent runs into rules.\n\n**Model priors and outdated principles**\n- Models may already know these principles, so a full book-sized rule set may not be better than a short reminder. The author shifted to framing `mini` as \"a short, decision-changing reminder.\"\n- Some principles may be dated in the AI coding era. The author filters content through an agent-oriented process but acknowledges the source material remains rooted in classic books.\n\n**Source and licensing concerns**\n- A commenter raised concerns about the origin of the distilled content. The author reframed the files as \"practical engineering instructions inspired by the books, not official materials,\" and rated the issue only partly addressed.\n- The project will not seek formal legal clearance: it \"will not attempt to provide formal legal clearance or legal analysis.\" I can't assess the legal questions here.","durationMs":9831,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books/main/docs/CRITICISM.md"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6ad02a3d-6a30-4ea2-9fa6-0b6fc24b1fdf","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"5152aad7-4e87-48ec-acc3-003a47393cc2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a64db6bd-a503-4549-bc43-76dbb357cf44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011U7gCpNwbZyypvFYWQ2LN4","type":"tool_result","content":"## Structure\n\nThe file is a Markdown document under an H1 title, with five H2 sections:\n\n- **When to use**: the kinds of systems the rules cover.\n- **Primary bias to correct**: a short statement of the mindset the rules push against.\n- **Decision rules**: general principles, as bullets.\n- **Trigger rules**: conditional rules that fire on a specific change or event.\n- **Final checklist**: yes/no questions for review.\n\n## Phrasing and Length\n\n- **Decision rules** are imperative or declarative statements (\"Put,\" \"Retry,\" \"Prefer,\" \"Treat\"). Each usually includes a rationale after a semicolon or in a \"so/instead of\" clause. Most run one to three lines, and some are long, packing several sub-directives into one bullet.\n- **Trigger rules** use an \"When [change], [do these things]\" pattern. Each lists a set of required definitions or actions, separated by commas, so they are the longest items in the file.\n- **Checklist items** are short questions ending in question marks.\n\n## Representative Rules\n\n1. \"Put explicit, intentional time limits on outbound calls and waits.\"\n2. \"Retry only when the operation is safe for the caller and provider; bound count and total time, use backoff or jitter\"\n3. \"Prefer designs that fail visibly, limit blast radius, shed load, preserve core service, and make diagnosis possible\"\n4. \"Assume every dependency, queue, cache, timeout, caller retry, and degraded state can fail in slow, partial,\"\n5. \"Isolate dependency and workload failures with circuit breakers, fast failure, bulkheads\"\n6. \"When adding an outbound call, dependency operation, resource checkout, queue consume, or thread wait, define timeout,\"\n7. \"When reviewing an incident, performance failure, or capacity issue, identify the failure chain, missing defenses,\"\n\n## Scoping and Applicability\n\n- There is no frontmatter, file-path glob, or language-specific scope. Applicability is described in prose under \"When to use,\" which covers services, APIs, jobs, queues, deployment paths, and control tooling.\n- Trigger rules scope by event instead of by location. They activate when a particular kind of change occurs, such as adding an outbound call, a queue, a health check, or an administrative control.\n- The checklist applies as a final review pass across all of the above."}]},"uuid":"48c8bac0-28a6-4630-8222-f4adc3f4e71e","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:19.788Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":6372,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Structure\n\nThe file is a Markdown document under an H1 title, with five H2 sections:\n\n- **When to use**: the kinds of systems the rules cover.\n- **Primary bias to correct**: a short statement of the mindset the rules push against.\n- **Decision rules**: general principles, as bullets.\n- **Trigger rules**: conditional rules that fire on a specific change or event.\n- **Final checklist**: yes/no questions for review.\n\n## Phrasing and Length\n\n- **Decision rules** are imperative or declarative statements (\"Put,\" \"Retry,\" \"Prefer,\" \"Treat\"). Each usually includes a rationale after a semicolon or in a \"so/instead of\" clause. Most run one to three lines, and some are long, packing several sub-directives into one bullet.\n- **Trigger rules** use an \"When [change], [do these things]\" pattern. Each lists a set of required definitions or actions, separated by commas, so they are the longest items in the file.\n- **Checklist items** are short questions ending in question marks.\n\n## Representative Rules\n\n1. \"Put explicit, intentional time limits on outbound calls and waits.\"\n2. \"Retry only when the operation is safe for the caller and provider; bound count and total time, use backoff or jitter\"\n3. \"Prefer designs that fail visibly, limit blast radius, shed load, preserve core service, and make diagnosis possible\"\n4. \"Assume every dependency, queue, cache, timeout, caller retry, and degraded state can fail in slow, partial,\"\n5. \"Isolate dependency and workload failures with circuit breakers, fast failure, bulkheads\"\n6. \"When adding an outbound call, dependency operation, resource checkout, queue consume, or thread wait, define timeout,\"\n7. \"When reviewing an incident, performance failure, or capacity issue, identify the failure chain, missing defenses,\"\n\n## Scoping and Applicability\n\n- There is no frontmatter, file-path glob, or language-specific scope. Applicability is described in prose under \"When to use,\" which covers services, APIs, jobs, queues, deployment paths, and control tooling.\n- Trigger rules scope by event instead of by location. They activate when a particular kind of change occurs, such as adding an outbound call, a queue, a health check, or an administrative control.\n- The checklist applies as a final review pass across all of the above.","durationMs":13450,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books/main/release-it/release-it.mini.md"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5152aad7-4e87-48ec-acc3-003a47393cc2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
