{"parentUuid":"414f99db-643b-4849-9488-c43ddeabf508","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["graphify: knowledge graph at graphify-out/. For focused questions, run `graphify query \"<question>\"` (scoped subgraph, usually much smaller than GRAPH_REPORT.md) instead of grepping raw files. Read GRAPH_REPORT.md only for broad architecture context."],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Monitor","toolUseID":"toolu_01Ep8CEet6nXpiFJquQmg3GD","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"96e31d44-8136-4f3a-8db5-70a40630ffeb","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:38.913Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Monitor hook additional context: graphify: knowledge graph at graphify-out/. For focused questions, run `graphify query \"<question>\"` (scoped subgraph, usually much smaller than GRAPH_REPORT.md) instead of grepping raw files. Read GRAPH_REPORT.md only for broad architecture context.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"44e4d8b3-dad6-4bf6-a789-b0083e186db4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a64db6bd-a503-4549-bc43-76dbb357cf44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ep8CEet6nXpiFJquQmg3GD","type":"tool_result","content":"Monitor started (task bx29nswhp, expires in 30m unless the source ends first; you get one notice at expiry — re-arm if you still need the watch). You will be notified on each event. Keep working — do not poll or sleep. Events may arrive while you are waiting for the user — an event is not their reply."}]},"uuid":"ce19ab91-f179-4633-892a-78effb435f00","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:39.083Z","toolUseResult":{"taskId":"bx29nswhp","timeoutMs":1800000,"persistent":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"44e4d8b3-dad6-4bf6-a789-b0083e186db4","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"c7aebbc9-4457-41f6-8978-d653feb5c437","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a64db6bd-a503-4549-bc43-76dbb357cf44","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CEN44yjEg8UoQhfJiDwo6D","type":"tool_result","content":"The process distills a source book into three tiers: a canonical `full.md`, an on-demand `mini.md`, and a stricter `nano.md` fallback. The goal is to preserve decisions, not sentences. The stated aim is rules that are \"decision-equivalent to the full source, not sentence-equivalent.\"\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. **Set the source of truth.** The canonical rules live in the main book directory and are exposed to each workbench as a symlinked `full.md`, which is never edited.\n2. **Classify each section** as `default`, `book-thesis`, `decision-changing`, `micro-decision`, `conflict-resolver`, `trigger`, `checklist-only`, or `framing` before compressing.\n3. **Build `mini.md`.** Keep every book-thesis, decision-changing, and conflict-resolver rule. Merge duplicates that have the same operational consequence, and turn long review or testing lists into triggers and a final checklist.\n4. **Build `nano.md`.** Keep only rules safe for an always-on, constrained context budget, such as corrections for known model biases. Be stricter here than in `mini.md`.\n5. **Write `traceability.md`.** Give every retained rule an `M*` or `N*` id tied to source section names and line ranges. Record every omission as covered by an `M*`, covered by an `N*`, or intentionally lost.\n6. **Run a section coverage review.** Assign every source section to one outcome: kept in `mini.md`, merged into `M*` rules, kept only in `nano.md`, or intentionally lost. This is mandatory.\n7. **Diagnose review findings** as either a process bug or a book-specific miss. Process bugs go into `PROCESS.md` first, and the book is re-run under the revised process rather than patched by hand.\n\n**Choosing decision-changing rules**\n\nA rule qualifies if it changes architecture, modeling, persistence, error handling, operations, or refactoring. Micro-decisions also qualify when agents commonly violate them. The removal test applies: \"if deleting a rule is likely to bring back a known bad habit, keep it.\" When unsure, the document says to keep the rule in `mini.md`.\n\n**Quality gates and evals**\n\n- A `default` label needs evidence: \"Require evidence from evals, review findings, or known model mistakes.\" Human-obvious is not the same as agent-default.\n- Validation checks cover H1 format, untouched `full.md`, traceable dispositions for every omission, merged duplicates, `nano.md` standing alone, and `mini.md` adding real value beyond `nano.md`.\n- Omissions cannot be dismissed vaguely. The document forbids describing them only as \"secondary\" or \"lower-yield\" and requires showing where the operational effect survived.\n\n**Traceability**\n\nEvery compressed rule maps back to a source section and line range. Each omitted or merged rule has a recorded disposition, so any removal can be audited back to `full.md`."}]},"uuid":"522067fa-9c72-43a4-9aa7-642a614200ba","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:11:44.947Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":11283,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The process distills a source book into three tiers: a canonical `full.md`, an on-demand `mini.md`, and a stricter `nano.md` fallback. The goal is to preserve decisions, not sentences. The stated aim is rules that are \"decision-equivalent to the full source, not sentence-equivalent.\"\n\n**Steps**\n\n1. **Set the source of truth.** The canonical rules live in the main book directory and are exposed to each workbench as a symlinked `full.md`, which is never edited.\n2. **Classify each section** as `default`, `book-thesis`, `decision-changing`, `micro-decision`, `conflict-resolver`, `trigger`, `checklist-only`, or `framing` before compressing.\n3. **Build `mini.md`.** Keep every book-thesis, decision-changing, and conflict-resolver rule. Merge duplicates that have the same operational consequence, and turn long review or testing lists into triggers and a final checklist.\n4. **Build `nano.md`.** Keep only rules safe for an always-on, constrained context budget, such as corrections for known model biases. Be stricter here than in `mini.md`.\n5. **Write `traceability.md`.** Give every retained rule an `M*` or `N*` id tied to source section names and line ranges. Record every omission as covered by an `M*`, covered by an `N*`, or intentionally lost.\n6. **Run a section coverage review.** Assign every source section to one outcome: kept in `mini.md`, merged into `M*` rules, kept only in `nano.md`, or intentionally lost. This is mandatory.\n7. **Diagnose review findings** as either a process bug or a book-specific miss. Process bugs go into `PROCESS.md` first, and the book is re-run under the revised process rather than patched by hand.\n\n**Choosing decision-changing rules**\n\nA rule qualifies if it changes architecture, modeling, persistence, error handling, operations, or refactoring. Micro-decisions also qualify when agents commonly violate them. The removal test applies: \"if deleting a rule is likely to bring back a known bad habit, keep it.\" When unsure, the document says to keep the rule in `mini.md`.\n\n**Quality gates and evals**\n\n- A `default` label needs evidence: \"Require evidence from evals, review findings, or known model mistakes.\" Human-obvious is not the same as agent-default.\n- Validation checks cover H1 format, untouched `full.md`, traceable dispositions for every omission, merged duplicates, `nano.md` standing alone, and `mini.md` adding real value beyond `nano.md`.\n- Omissions cannot be dismissed vaguely. The document forbids describing them only as \"secondary\" or \"lower-yield\" and requires showing where the operational effect survived.\n\n**Traceability**\n\nEvery compressed rule maps back to a source section and line range. Each omitted or merged rule has a recorded disposition, so any removal can be audited back to `full.md`.","durationMs":8143,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattpocock/agent-rules-books/main/_rule-workbench/PROCESS.md"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c7aebbc9-4457-41f6-8978-d653feb5c437","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
