{"parentUuid":"522067fa-9c72-43a4-9aa7-642a614200ba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4mfAARPy4ms8dm7MHvK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1864,"cache_read_input_tokens":374015,"output_tokens":2600,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1448},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1864,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2600,"cache_read_input_tokens":374015,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1864,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1864},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":16822,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch4mf9unXyEvGuw4fJNVu","requestRef":"f3c77057-25f2-4ebf-b195-910ef7f21311","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"a669845f-5311-40e0-bdea-b8703028c66b","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:12:02.679Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"a669845f-5311-40e0-bdea-b8703028c66b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4mfAARPy4ms8dm7MHvK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The repo is a fork of `ciembor/agent-rules-books` (MIT). It holds 15 rule sets distilled from software books, each in `full`, `mini` and `nano` tiers. I read it through a page summarizer and not the raw files, so treat the quoted phrasings as paraphrase.\n\n**What it gets right, and how it applies to us**\n- **Rules loaded only when needed.** The repo says to keep one small always-on file and move everything else to skills, scoped rules or retrieval. I didn't apply that to `AGENTS.md` in #527: the XERJ section is about 2 KB, matters only for non-trivial code, and could sit in an on-demand doc behind a one-line trigger.\n- **Claude Code doesn't read `AGENTS.md`.** Its guidance is a short `CLAUDE.md` that imports `AGENTS.md` with `@AGENTS.md`. This repo has only `AGENTS.md`, and the repo rules never appeared in this session's context at startup. I haven't confirmed that in Claude Code's docs. A one-line `CLAUDE.md` would fix it if it's true.\n- **Rules that trigger on events.** Each file has \"When to use\", a \"primary bias to correct\" line, decision rules, \"When [event], do X\" trigger rules, and a final yes/no checklist. Our merge and release rules in `AGENTS.md` are really triggers (\"when tagging a stable…\") and would read better in that shape.\n- **A removal test.** A rule stays only if deleting it would bring back a known bad habit. A rule labelled \"default\" needs evidence from evals or review findings. This matches the no-op pruning from the writing-for-agents skill.\n- **Fix the process, not the one case.** When a review finds a miss, they update `PROCESS.md` first and re-run the distillation, and every compressed rule traces back to its source. That is the same loop as our `tasks/lessons.md`.\n\n**Where we're already ahead**\n- **Incident-derived rules.** The author admits their rules are \"primarily book-derived, not incident-derived\". Ours come from real failures this release, such as the stale merge refs, the tag-before-main slip, and the batch merge that broke `main`.\n- **Measured effects.** They have no outcome evals. Their one signal is a single ChatGPT-rated refactor (about 74/100 with the rules against about 46 with only a book name), which they call early and qualitative. We have a CERI evaluation harness, so we could A/B an instruction file on real scenarios.\n\n**What to leave**\n- **Book-derived architecture rules.** Clean Code, DDD and similar sets risk over-engineering and pseudo-compliance, and their own critics say so. The author also notes there is no conflict matrix between books and no way to enforce task selection. Our value is the repo-specific rules an agent can't look up.\n- **Licensing.** The distilled content is \"inspired by\" the books, and the author declined to seek legal clearance. I wouldn't copy rules from it.\n\n**Concrete next steps**\n1. Add a one-line `CLAUDE.md` with `@AGENTS.md`.\n2. Move the XERJ block to an on-demand doc, and give the merge and release rules a \"primary bias\" line plus a final checklist.\n3. Later, test whether an instruction file changes outcomes using CERI.\n\nI can fold steps 1 and 2 into #527 if you want; I haven't changed anything yet. The monitor for #526, #519 and #527 is still running."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1864,"cache_read_input_tokens":374015,"output_tokens":2600,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1448},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1864,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2600,"cache_read_input_tokens":374015,"cache_creation_input_