{"parentUuid":"66b4e4d3-610a-4dde-b2c1-3f6c36270c3e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4nLzuBD4biKZJVBXV7w","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WuAKoDmmuekFgEEmJBvNrj","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk","prompt":"Summarize the SDK documentation structure and key concepts: agents, tools, conversation persistence, event system, workspace types, security (confirmation policy, risk analyzer), context condenser, skills, sub-agent delegation, stuck detection. Note any concrete design decisions."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25,"cache_read_input_tokens":387745,"output_tokens":790,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":188},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":790,"cache_read_input_tokens":387745,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":1015,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WuAKoDmmuekFgEEmJBvNrj":{"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk","prompt":"Summarize the SDK documentation structure and key concepts: agents, tools, conversation persistence, event system, workspace types, security (confirmation policy, risk analyzer), context condenser, skills, sub-agent delegation, stuck detection. Note any concrete design decisions."}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011Ch4nLzc4HYYM4ksTwfokt","requestRef":"ac74479b-c0d5-4a5e-b150-68ae147a0080","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"de56af5d-eaaa-4109-a75c-94496dda0a8e","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:20:52.353Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"b25b2ae6-307c-4172-84f7-3df7b9e05918","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CiVdJrsWxwZ5tKAzWg8Yem","type":"tool_result","content":"## OpenHands (Agent Canvas) at a glance\n\nThe page describes OpenHands as **\"The self-hosted developer control center for coding agents and automations.\"** The README's title is now \"Agent Canvas.\" It runs the open-source OpenHands agent by default and can also drive third-party agents such as Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Pi, and OpenCode, via the Agent-Client Protocol (ACP).\n\n## Components and architecture\n\n- **Agent SDK / Agent Server:** The OpenHands Agent Server is a REST API for running agents. It lives in the separate `software-agent-sdk` repo, which also holds agents, tools, conversations, and workspaces.\n- **Frontend (GUI):** The Agent Canvas web UI, served at `localhost:8000`, which can connect to multiple agent backends.\n- **CLI / launcher:** The `agent-canvas` command, installed with `npm install -g @openhands/agent-canvas`. Flags such as `--frontend-only` and `--backend-only` split the stack.\n- **Runtime / sandbox:** Agents can run directly on the host (with full filesystem access), in one Docker sandbox, or with one Docker container per conversation (`OH_CONVERSATION_RUNTIME=docker`).\n- **Automation server:** A separate `automation` repo handles scheduling, webhooks, and dispatching work to the Agent Server.\n- **Cloud / Enterprise:** Agents can optionally run on OpenHands Cloud or OpenHands Enterprise infrastructure.\n- **Resolver and evaluation:** The page does not mention either of these.\n\n## Licensing\n\nThe page shows an MIT license. It does not mention any separate enterprise directories or license split, so I can't confirm one from this content.\n\n## Notable features\n\n- Self-hosting on laptops, VMs, Docker, or cloud servers\n- Switching between local, remote, and cloud backends\n- Automations triggered on a schedule or by webhooks, integrating with Slack, GitHub, Linear, and Notion\n- Bring-your-own LLM, with LLM profiles\n- Multi-agent support through ACP\n\n## Top-level directories\n\n`.agents/skills`, `.github`, `.husky`, `.openhands`, `.pr`, `.screenshots`, `__mocks__`, `__tests__`, `bin`, `config`, `docker`, `docs`, `electron`, `examples/acp-docker`, `helm/agent-canvas`, `public`, `scripts`, `src`, `test-evidence`, `tests/e2e`, `tools`\n\n## Linked sub-repos and packages\n\n- `OpenHands/software-agent-sdk`: Python SDK and Agent Server\n- `OpenHands/typescript-client`: browser-compatible TypeScript client for the Agent Server API\n- `OpenHands/automation`: automation definitions, scheduling, and dispatching\n- npm package `@openhands/agent-canvas`"}]},"uuid":"bb6dc790-472e-48da-a8ea-2493d07ef380","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:20:58.990Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":398720,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## OpenHands (Agent Canvas) at a glance\n\nThe page describes OpenHands as **\"The self-hosted developer control center for coding agents and automations.\"** The README's title is now \"Agent Canvas.\" It runs the open-source OpenHands agent by default and can also drive third-party agents such as Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Pi, and OpenCode, via the Agent-Client Protocol (ACP).\n\n## Components and architecture\n\n- **Agent SDK / Agent Server:** The OpenHands Agent Server is a REST API for running agents. It lives in the separate `software-agent-sdk` repo, which also holds agents, tools, conversations, and workspaces.\n- **Frontend (GUI):** The Agent Canvas web UI, served at `localhost:8000`, which can connect to multiple agent backends.\n- **CLI / launcher:** The `agent-canvas` command, installed with `npm install -g @openhands/agent-canvas`. Flags such as `--frontend-only` and `--backend-only` split the stack.\n- **Runtime / sandbox:** Agents can run directly on the host (with full filesystem access), in one Docker sandbox, or with one Docker container per conversation (`OH_CONVERSATION_RUNTIME=docker`).\n- **Automation server:** A separate `automation` repo handles scheduling, webhooks, and dispatching work to the Agent Server.\n- **Cloud / Enterprise:** Agents can optionally run on OpenHands Cloud or OpenHands Enterprise infrastructure.\n- **Resolver and evaluation:** The page does not mention either of these.\n\n## Licensing\n\nThe page shows an MIT license. It does not mention any separate enterprise directories or license split, so I can't confirm one from this content.\n\n## Notable features\n\n- Self-hosting on laptops, VMs, Docker, or cloud servers\n- Switching between local, remote, and cloud backends\n- Automations triggered on a schedule or by webhooks, integrating with Slack, GitHub, Linear, and Notion\n- Bring-your-own LLM, with LLM profiles\n- Multi-agent support through ACP\n\n## Top-level directories\n\n`.agents/skills`, `.github`, `.husky`, `.openhands`, `.pr`, `.screenshots`, `__mocks__`, `__tests__`, `bin`, `config`, `docker`, `docs`, `electron`, `examples/acp-docker`, `helm/agent-canvas`, `public`, `scripts`, `src`, `test-evidence`, `tests/e2e`, `tools`\n\n## Linked sub-repos and packages\n\n- `OpenHands/software-agent-sdk`: Python SDK and Agent Server\n- `OpenHands/typescript-client`: browser-compatible TypeScript client for the Agent Server API\n- `OpenHands/automation`: automation definitions, scheduling, and dispatching\n- npm package `@openhands/agent-canvas`","durationMs":9225,"url":"https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b25b2ae6-307c-4172-84f7-3df7b9e05918","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"66b4e4d3-610a-4dde-b2c1-3f6c36270c3e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SSJXT1ktKbUX9jAEpnU2iM","type":"tool_result","content":"The page is the GitHub README and repo landing page for the OpenHands Software Agent SDK. It describes the SDK at a high level, but it doesn't document most of the internals you asked about.\n\n**Overview**\nThe SDK provides \"Python, TypeScript, and REST APIs for **building agents that work with code**.\" Agents can use the local machine as their workspace or run in ephemeral workspaces, such as Docker or Kubernetes, through the Agent Server. It is the engine behind the OpenHands CLI and OpenHands Cloud.\n\n**Architecture (as described)**\n- **Agent and tools:** A quick-start example creates an `Agent` with an `LLM` and tools: `TerminalTool`, `FileEditorTool`, and `TaskTrackerTool`.\n- **Conversation:** A `Conversation` is bound to a workspace, receives messages via `send_message`, and executes via `run()`.\n- **Workspace:** Local or sandboxed (Docker/Kubernetes via Agent Server).\n- **API layer:** The repo owns \"agents, tools, conversations, workspaces, events, the REST/WebSocket API, and typed client access.\"\n- **Client flow:** \"SDK/Agent Server → OpenAPI contract → `clients/typescript` → Agent Canvas.\"\n- **Skills:** `AgentContext(load_public_skills=True)` loads public skills from the `OpenHands/extensions` marketplace, such as `uv` and `deno`.\n- **MCP:** Only mentioned as a guide topic in the docs list.\n\n**Not covered on the page**\nThe following are absent: the event log internals, the LLM layer beyond the `LLM` class, the condenser or context management, the security analyzer and confirmation policy, stuck detection, iterative refinement, and the critic.\n\n**Design principles**\nThe page describes the SDK as \"A clean, modular SDK\" and lists \"Repository boundaries,\" with backend behavior kept in the Python SDK or Agent Server and browser client access in `clients/typescript`. It doesn't state other explicit principles.\n\n**Packages and directories**\n- `openhands-sdk`: core Python SDK\n- `openhands-tools`: tools\n- `openhands-agent-server`: Agent Server\n- `openhands-workspace`: workspaces\n- `clients/typescript`: browser-compatible TypeScript client\n- `examples/`: standalone SDK, remote Agent Server, GitHub workflows, and skills/plugins examples\n- `tests/`, `scripts/`, `.github/`, `.agents/skills`, `.openhands/`, `.pr/`, `.impeccable/`\n\nDevelopment setup is via `make build` and `DEVELOPMENT.md`. The project is MIT-licensed."}]},"uuid":"0b6824b8-3a9a-4074-9057-60f9e0def03a","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:00.089Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":330905,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page is the GitHub README and repo landing page for the OpenHands Software Agent SDK. It describes the SDK at a high level, but it doesn't document most of the internals you asked about.\n\n**Overview**\nThe SDK provides \"Python, TypeScript, and REST APIs for **building agents that work with code**.\" Agents can use the local machine as their workspace or run in ephemeral workspaces, such as Docker or Kubernetes, through the Agent Server. It is the engine behind the OpenHands CLI and OpenHands Cloud.\n\n**Architecture (as described)**\n- **Agent and tools:** A quick-start example creates an `Agent` with an `LLM` and tools: `TerminalTool`, `FileEditorTool`, and `TaskTrackerTool`.\n- **Conversation:** A `Conversation` is bound to a workspace, receives messages via `send_message`, and executes via `run()`.\n- **Workspace:** Local or sandboxed (Docker/Kubernetes via Agent Server).\n- **API layer:** The repo owns \"agents, tools, conversations, workspaces, events, the REST/WebSocket API, and typed client access.\"\n- **Client flow:** \"SDK/Agent Server → OpenAPI contract → `clients/typescript` → Agent Canvas.\"\n- **Skills:** `AgentContext(load_public_skills=True)` loads public skills from the `OpenHands/extensions` marketplace, such as `uv` and `deno`.\n- **MCP:** Only mentioned as a guide topic in the docs list.\n\n**Not covered on the page**\nThe following are absent: the event log internals, the LLM layer beyond the `LLM` class, the condenser or context management, the security analyzer and confirmation policy, stuck detection, iterative refinement, and the critic.\n\n**Design principles**\nThe page describes the SDK as \"A clean, modular SDK\" and lists \"Repository boundaries,\" with backend behavior kept in the Python SDK or Agent Server and browser client access in `clients/typescript`. It doesn't state other explicit principles.\n\n**Packages and directories**\n- `openhands-sdk`: core Python SDK\n- `openhands-tools`: tools\n- `openhands-agent-server`: Agent Server\n- `openhands-workspace`: workspaces\n- `clients/typescript`: browser-compatible TypeScript client\n- `examples/`: standalone SDK, remote Agent Server, GitHub workflows, and skills/plugins examples\n- `tests/`, `scripts/`, `.github/`, `.agents/skills`, `.openhands/`, `.pr/`, `.impeccable/`\n\nDevelopment setup is via `make build` and `DEVELOPMENT.md`. The project is MIT-licensed.","durationMs":8678,"url":"https://github.com/OpenHands/software-agent-sdk"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"66b4e4d3-610a-4dde-b2c1-3f6c36270c3e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"de56af5d-eaaa-4109-a75c-94496dda0a8e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WuAKoDmmuekFgEEmJBvNrj","type":"tool_result","content":"The page is mostly a landing page. It gives the SDK's purpose, feature list, and doc structure, but it doesn't explain most of the concepts you listed.\n\n**Purpose**\nThe SDK is described as a set of Python and REST APIs for building \"agents that work with code.\" It targets coding work specifically, not general chat or back-office automation.\n\n**Documentation structure**\n- **Get Started:** a Getting Started guide covering installation and a first agent run.\n- **Learn the SDK:** a Core Concepts page (architecture: agents, tools, workspaces, and more) and an API reference that points to the source code.\n- **Build with Examples:** standalone local agents with custom tools, remote execution with Docker sandboxing, and GitHub workflows for repository automation.\n- **Community:** Slack and GitHub.\n\n**Features**\n- A single Python API for running agents locally or in the cloud, defining custom behaviors, and creating custom tools.\n- Pre-defined tools for Bash, file editing, web browsing, and MCP integration.\n- A REST-based agent server that can run in Docker or Kubernetes and connects to the Python API.\n- OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions and Responses endpoints.\n\n**Concrete design decisions**\n- **Model-agnostic:** any LLM can be used, including proprietary and open-source models.\n- **Local and remote share one API:** the same Python interface works locally or against the agent server.\n- **Sandboxed remote execution:** remote runs use Docker sandboxing.\n- **Open source:** MIT-licensed.\n- **Stated feature focus:** task planning and decomposition, automatic context compression, security analysis, and agent-computer interfaces.\n\n**Not covered on this page**\nThe page doesn't describe agent internals, conversation persistence, the event system, workspace types, confirmation policies, the risk analyzer, the context condenser, skills, sub-agent delegation, or stuck detection. The Core Concepts page (`/sdk/arch/overview`) is the most likely place for those details, and the linked guides may cover them as well. If you share that content, I can summarize those topics."}]},"uuid":"1e3525d1-2cae-4009-bf39-75065258bbdc","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:00.321Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":4706,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page is mostly a landing page. It gives the SDK's purpose, feature list, and doc structure, but it doesn't explain most of the concepts you listed.\n\n**Purpose**\nThe SDK is described as a set of Python and REST APIs for building \"agents that work with code.\" It targets coding work specifically, not general chat or back-office automation.\n\n**Documentation structure**\n- **Get Started:** a Getting Started guide covering installation and a first agent run.\n- **Learn the SDK:** a Core Concepts page (architecture: agents, tools, workspaces, and more) and an API reference that points to the source code.\n- **Build with Examples:** standalone local agents with custom tools, remote execution with Docker sandboxing, and GitHub workflows for repository automation.\n- **Community:** Slack and GitHub.\n\n**Features**\n- A single Python API for running agents locally or in the cloud, defining custom behaviors, and creating custom tools.\n- Pre-defined tools for Bash, file editing, web browsing, and MCP integration.\n- A REST-based agent server that can run in Docker or Kubernetes and connects to the Python API.\n- OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions and Responses endpoints.\n\n**Concrete design decisions**\n- **Model-agnostic:** any LLM can be used, including proprietary and open-source models.\n- **Local and remote share one API:** the same Python interface works locally or against the agent server.\n- **Sandboxed remote execution:** remote runs use Docker sandboxing.\n- **Open source:** MIT-licensed.\n- **Stated feature focus:** task planning and decomposition, automatic context compression, security analysis, and agent-computer interfaces.\n\n**Not covered on this page**\nThe page doesn't describe agent internals, conversation persistence, the event system, workspace types, confirmation policies, the risk analyzer, the context condenser, skills, sub-agent delegation, or stuck detection. The Core Concepts page (`/sdk/arch/overview`) is the most likely place for those details, and the linked guides may cover them as well. If you share that content, I can summarize those topics.","durationMs":7863,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"de56af5d-eaaa-4109-a75c-94496dda0a8e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
