{"parentUuid":"944becdc-6691-4756-a3a1-1d3c43c88790","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4nN6ptzE6XAJbwbdQ9V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0196H3sFgRYzMpPL9aXaCzAS","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/design","prompt":"Summarize the design principles and decisions of the OpenHands SDK: what they chose and why (e.g. V0 to V1 redesign lessons, modularity, event-sourcing, optional isolation, stateless components, security). Quote key points briefly."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3471,"cache_read_input_tokens":387770,"output_tokens":490,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":95},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3471,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":490,"cache_read_input_tokens":387770,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3471},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":1037,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0196H3sFgRYzMpPL9aXaCzAS":{"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/design","prompt":"Summarize the design principles and decisions of the OpenHands SDK: what they chose and why (e.g. V0 to V1 redesign lessons, modularity, event-sourcing, optional isolation, stateless components, security). Quote key points briefly."}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Ch4nN6XnShd4gCgkWhUPF","requestRef":"0993cfe0-725b-44e5-a02f-fc95f2803cde","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"6398be8e-3b9b-4fb5-91d3-b52c0babdc82","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:04.333Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"6398be8e-3b9b-4fb5-91d3-b52c0babdc82","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196H3sFgRYzMpPL9aXaCzAS","type":"tool_result","content":"The OpenHands Software Agent SDK is part of OpenHands V1, a rework of V0 based on what the project learned as it grew. The page names four principles.\n\n**1. Optional isolation instead of mandatory sandboxing**\nV0 ran every tool call in a Docker container by default. That made agent and sandbox separate processes whose states could drift apart, and multi-tenant workloads could crash each other. V1 runs agent and tools in one process by default, which fits the Model Context Protocol's local-execution model. Containerization remains available when isolation is needed. The principle is \"Sandboxing should be opt-in, not universal.\"\n\n**2. Stateless components with one source of truth for state**\nV0's mutable objects and dynamic typing caused silent inconsistencies, such as failed session restores and version drift. In V1, agents, tools, LLMs, and configurations are immutable Pydantic models validated at construction. The only mutable entity is the conversation state, which the page says enables deterministic replay and reliable persistence. The page frames this as \"Keep everything stateless, with exactly one mutable state.\" It doesn't use the term event sourcing, though the single state record and replay behavior point that way.\n\n**3. Clear boundaries between the agent and applications**\nV0 used one codebase for the CLI, web UI, and integrations, so app-specific conditionals and prompts crept into the agent core. V1 splits the system into the SDK (agent core), tools, workspace (sandbox), and agent server. Applications talk to the agent through APIs rather than embedding it, so research and production can evolve independently. The principle is \"Maintain strict separation of concerns.\"\n\n**4. Composable components for extensibility**\nHard-coded agent logic in V0 meant new tools or entry points required branching logic. In V1, agents are graphs of interchangeable, strongly typed components such as tools, prompts, LLMs, and contexts. Developers can swap toolsets, override prompts, or add delegation logic without changing core code. The principle is \"Everything should be composable and safe to extend.\"\n\nThe page doesn't discuss security in detail beyond the isolation model."}]},"uuid":"e0094808-81d6-4a1e-8730-14ef2586b510","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:10.711Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":4753,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The OpenHands Software Agent SDK is part of OpenHands V1, a rework of V0 based on what the project learned as it grew. The page names four principles.\n\n**1. Optional isolation instead of mandatory sandboxing**\nV0 ran every tool call in a Docker container by default. That made agent and sandbox separate processes whose states could drift apart, and multi-tenant workloads could crash each other. V1 runs agent and tools in one process by default, which fits the Model Context Protocol's local-execution model. Containerization remains available when isolation is needed. The principle is \"Sandboxing should be opt-in, not universal.\"\n\n**2. Stateless components with one source of truth for state**\nV0's mutable objects and dynamic typing caused silent inconsistencies, such as failed session restores and version drift. In V1, agents, tools, LLMs, and configurations are immutable Pydantic models validated at construction. The only mutable entity is the conversation state, which the page says enables deterministic replay and reliable persistence. The page frames this as \"Keep everything stateless, with exactly one mutable state.\" It doesn't use the term event sourcing, though the single state record and replay behavior point that way.\n\n**3. Clear boundaries between the agent and applications**\nV0 used one codebase for the CLI, web UI, and integrations, so app-specific conditionals and prompts crept into the agent core. V1 splits the system into the SDK (agent core), tools, workspace (sandbox), and agent server. Applications talk to the agent through APIs rather than embedding it, so research and production can evolve independently. The principle is \"Maintain strict separation of concerns.\"\n\n**4. Composable components for extensibility**\nHard-coded agent logic in V0 meant new tools or entry points required branching logic. In V1, agents are graphs of interchangeable, strongly typed components such as tools, prompts, LLMs, and contexts. Developers can swap toolsets, override prompts, or add delegation logic without changing core code. The principle is \"Everything should be composable and safe to extend.\"\n\nThe page doesn't discuss security in detail beyond the isolation model.","durationMs":6093,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/design"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6398be8e-3b9b-4fb5-91d3-b52c0babdc82","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"944becdc-6691-4756-a3a1-1d3c43c88790","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J79kQkkkwXpVJH9vmkYdvX","type":"tool_result","content":"## Overview\n\nThe OpenHands Software Agent SDK is a framework for building and deploying AI agents, from local experiments to production systems. The overview emphasizes three goals: statelessness, composability, and clear boundaries between research and deployment.\n\n## Relationship to OpenHands Applications\n\n- **The SDK defines agent behavior.** It provides agents, LLMs, conversations, tools, workspaces, events, and security policies. The document states that \"The Software Agent SDK is the source of truth for agents in OpenHands.\"\n- **Agent Server exposes remote execution.** It is in the same repository and serves SDK conversations and workspaces to remote clients through REST and WebSocket APIs.\n- **Applications live elsewhere.** Agent Canvas, the OpenHands CLI, and custom clients consume the SDK or Agent Server from separate repositories.\n\n## Package Structure\n\n| Package | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `openhands.sdk` | Core agent framework and base workspace classes (required) |\n| `openhands.tools` | Pre-built tools such as bash and file editing (optional) |\n| `openhands.workspace` | Extended workspaces such as Docker and remote API (optional) |\n| `openhands.agent_server` | Multi-user FastAPI server (optional) |\n\n## Core SDK Components\n\n- **Agent:** Implements the reasoning-action loop.\n- **Conversation:** Manages conversation state and lifecycle.\n- **LLM:** A provider-agnostic interface with retry and telemetry.\n- **Tool System:** Typed base classes for action, observation, tool, and executor, plus MCP integration.\n- **Events:** A typed event framework covering actions, observations, user messages, and state updates.\n- **Workspace:** Base classes `Workspace`, `LocalWorkspace`, and `RemoteWorkspace`.\n- **Skill:** Reusable user-defined prompts with trigger-based activation.\n- **Condenser:** Compresses conversation history to manage tokens.\n- **Security:** Assesses action risk and validates actions before execution.\n\n## Execution Flow\n\nA user message goes to the Conversation, which passes it to the Agent. The Agent asks the LLM what to do. The LLM may choose a tool, such as running a bash command. The Agent executes the tool, returns the observation to the LLM, and repeats until the task is done. The Agent then updates conversation state and reports back.\n\n## Design Principles\n\n- **Statelessness and immutability:** The document describes the SDK's components as \"Stateless, immutable components with type-safe Pydantic models.\"\n- **Type safety:** Pydantic models and typed events and tool patterns are used throughout.\n- **Composability:** Components are designed to be combined, and the same agent code can run in different environments.\n- **Separation of concerns:** Agent behavior is kept distinct from where execution happens.\n\n## Workspace and Sandbox Model\n\n- **Local mode:** Install `openhands-sdk` and `openhands-tools`. Everything runs in one process with `LocalWorkspace`, requiring no Docker.\n- **Production/sandboxed mode:** Install all four packages. `DockerWorkspace` and `RemoteAPIWorkspace` both extend `RemoteWorkspace`. The Docker option spawns a container running Agent Server, while the remote option connects over HTTP.\n- **Portability:** \"Same agent code works in both modes.\" Switching workspace types changes deployment without changing agent code.\n\nTools run in whatever environment the workspace configures. In the document's words, \"Tools run alongside the agent in whatever environment workspace configures.\" Tools do not run through workspace APIs.\n\n## Tool and Security Model\n\n- All tools follow an Action/Observation/Executor pattern with built-in validation, error handling, and security.\n- The `openhands.tools` package provides pre-built tools. The source repository is the authoritative list.\n- The Security component performs action risk assessment and validation before execution.\n\n## Agent Server\n\n- REST and WebSocket endpoints for conversations, bash, files, events, desktop, and VS Code.\n- OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/responses` endpoints.\n- Isolated per-user sessions, API key authentication, and health checks.\n- Runs inside containers via `DockerWorkspace` or as a standalone process via `RemoteWorkspace`.\n\n## Not Covered in This Overview\n\nThe following topics are not addressed in the provided content, so I can't summarize them from it:\n\n- **Event sourcing and deterministic replay:** The document describes a typed event framework but does not say whether state is derived from an event log or whether runs can be replayed.\n- **Stuck detection:** Not mentioned.\n- **Sub-agents:** Not mentioned.\n\nThe per-component pages linked from the overview (such as Events, Conversation, and Condenser) would be the place to check these."}]},"uuid":"6ddcc323-5aa7-44cc-8238-82a2b39a7779","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:15.099Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":13809,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Overview\n\nThe OpenHands Software Agent SDK is a framework for building and deploying AI agents, from local experiments to production systems. The overview emphasizes three goals: statelessness, composability, and clear boundaries between research and deployment.\n\n## Relationship to OpenHands Applications\n\n- **The SDK defines agent behavior.** It provides agents, LLMs, conversations, tools, workspaces, events, and security policies. The document states that \"The Software Agent SDK is the source of truth for agents in OpenHands.\"\n- **Agent Server exposes remote execution.** It is in the same repository and serves SDK conversations and workspaces to remote clients through REST and WebSocket APIs.\n- **Applications live elsewhere.** Agent Canvas, the OpenHands CLI, and custom clients consume the SDK or Agent Server from separate repositories.\n\n## Package Structure\n\n| Package | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `openhands.sdk` | Core agent framework and base workspace classes (required) |\n| `openhands.tools` | Pre-built tools such as bash and file editing (optional) |\n| `openhands.workspace` | Extended workspaces such as Docker and remote API (optional) |\n| `openhands.agent_server` | Multi-user FastAPI server (optional) |\n\n## Core SDK Components\n\n- **Agent:** Implements the reasoning-action loop.\n- **Conversation:** Manages conversation state and lifecycle.\n- **LLM:** A provider-agnostic interface with retry and telemetry.\n- **Tool System:** Typed base classes for action, observation, tool, and executor, plus MCP integration.\n- **Events:** A typed event framework covering actions, observations, user messages, and state updates.\n- **Workspace:** Base classes `Workspace`, `LocalWorkspace`, and `RemoteWorkspace`.\n- **Skill:** Reusable user-defined prompts with trigger-based activation.\n- **Condenser:** Compresses conversation history to manage tokens.\n- **Security:** Assesses action risk and validates actions before execution.\n\n## Execution Flow\n\nA user message goes to the Conversation, which passes it to the Agent. The Agent asks the LLM what to do. The LLM may choose a tool, such as running a bash command. The Agent executes the tool, returns the observation to the LLM, and repeats until the task is done. The Agent then updates conversation state and reports back.\n\n## Design Principles\n\n- **Statelessness and immutability:** The document describes the SDK's components as \"Stateless, immutable components with type-safe Pydantic models.\"\n- **Type safety:** Pydantic models and typed events and tool patterns are used throughout.\n- **Composability:** Components are designed to be combined, and the same agent code can run in different environments.\n- **Separation of concerns:** Agent behavior is kept distinct from where execution happens.\n\n## Workspace and Sandbox Model\n\n- **Local mode:** Install `openhands-sdk` and `openhands-tools`. Everything runs in one process with `LocalWorkspace`, requiring no Docker.\n- **Production/sandboxed mode:** Install all four packages. `DockerWorkspace` and `RemoteAPIWorkspace` both extend `RemoteWorkspace`. The Docker option spawns a container running Agent Server, while the remote option connects over HTTP.\n- **Portability:** \"Same agent code works in both modes.\" Switching workspace types changes deployment without changing agent code.\n\nTools run in whatever environment the workspace configures. In the document's words, \"Tools run alongside the agent in whatever environment workspace configures.\" Tools do not run through workspace APIs.\n\n## Tool and Security Model\n\n- All tools follow an Action/Observation/Executor pattern with built-in validation, error handling, and security.\n- The `openhands.tools` package provides pre-built tools. The source repository is the authoritative list.\n- The Security component performs action risk assessment and validation before execution.\n\n## Agent Server\n\n- REST and WebSocket endpoints for conversations, bash, files, events, desktop, and VS Code.\n- OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/responses` endpoints.\n- Isolated per-user sessions, API key authentication, and health checks.\n- Runs inside containers via `DockerWorkspace` or as a standalone process via `RemoteWorkspace`.\n\n## Not Covered in This Overview\n\nThe following topics are not addressed in the provided content, so I can't summarize them from it:\n\n- **Event sourcing and deterministic replay:** The document describes a typed event framework but does not say whether state is derived from an event log or whether runs can be replayed.\n- **Stuck detection:** Not mentioned.\n- **Sub-agents:** Not mentioned.\n\nThe per-component pages linked from the overview (such as Events, Conversation, and Condenser) would be the place to check these.","durationMs":11762,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/overview"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"944becdc-6691-4756-a3a1-1d3c43c88790","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
