{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","prNumber":541,"prUrl":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/541","prRepository":"mikeholownych/nexus-code","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:22.848Z"}
{"parentUuid":"be2528d5-3919-46b1-b86f-bee506939f8e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cma1MP31HvQ2PBcmrj22JJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Stuck detection is a built-in feature that flags when an agent is looping or making no progress. It is enabled by default (`stuck_detection=True`).\n\n**How it works:** The detector analyzes the event history after the last user message. It compares events by semantic content, so actions are matched by tool name, content, and thought, while IDs and metrics are ignored.\n\n**Patterns and thresholds:**\n- **Repeating action-observation cycles:** the same action yields the same observation 4 or more times.\n- **Repeating action-error cycles:** the same action produces errors 3 or more times.\n- **Agent monologue:** the agent sends 3 or more consecutive messages without user input or meaningful progress.\n- **Alternating patterns:** two different action-observation pairs alternate in a ping-pong pattern for 6 or more cycles.\n- **Context window errors:** repeated context window errors signal memory management problems.\n\n**When stuck is detected:** In real time, the detector can automatically halt execution, which prevents infinite loops and wasted resources. Applications can also handle the flag themselves by stopping or taking corrective action. You can check the status at any point with `conversation.stuck_detector.is_stuck()`.\n\n**Configuration:** It is controlled through the `stuck_detection` parameter when creating a `Conversation`. It defaults to `True`, and the documentation's example sets it explicitly for clarity."}]},"uuid":"1fd53118-80f5-4cfa-bd67-eca4747afbc7","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:27.288Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":6465,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"Stuck detection is a built-in feature that flags when an agent is looping or making no progress. It is enabled by default (`stuck_detection=True`).\n\n**How it works:** The detector analyzes the event history after the last user message. It compares events by semantic content, so actions are matched by tool name, content, and thought, while IDs and metrics are ignored.\n\n**Patterns and thresholds:**\n- **Repeating action-observation cycles:** the same action yields the same observation 4 or more times.\n- **Repeating action-error cycles:** the same action produces errors 3 or more times.\n- **Agent monologue:** the agent sends 3 or more consecutive messages without user input or meaningful progress.\n- **Alternating patterns:** two different action-observation pairs alternate in a ping-pong pattern for 6 or more cycles.\n- **Context window errors:** repeated context window errors signal memory management problems.\n\n**When stuck is detected:** In real time, the detector can automatically halt execution, which prevents infinite loops and wasted resources. Applications can also handle the flag themselves by stopping or taking corrective action. You can check the status at any point with `conversation.stuck_detector.is_stuck()`.\n\n**Configuration:** It is controlled through the `stuck_detection` parameter when creating a `Conversation`. It defaults to `True`, and the documentation's example sets it explicitly for clarity.","durationMs":5711,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/guides/agent-stuck-detector"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be2528d5-3919-46b1-b86f-bee506939f8e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"46008d60-8330-42a8-8253-e967640d5237","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WwGNbZXyDEkCWu4mZKB4Uw","type":"tool_result","content":"## Security Architecture Summary\n\n**Purpose:** The system assesses agent actions for risk before execution. It uses pluggable analyzers and confirmation policies to decide when user approval is needed. It has four stated responsibilities: risk assessment, confirmation policy, action validation, and an audit trail of security decisions in event history.\n\n**Risk levels**\n- **LOW:** read-only, no state changes\n- **MEDIUM:** modifies user data\n- **HIGH:** dangerous operations such as deletion, system commands, or privilege escalation\n- **UNKNOWN:** not analyzed or indeterminate\n\n**Security analyzers**\n- **LLMSecurityAnalyzer** (inline assessment): A required `security_risk` parameter is added to non-read-only tool schemas. The LLM supplies a risk level with each tool call, and the analyzer returns it. The page states \"Risk assessment happens inline—no separate analysis step,\" so there are no extra LLM calls.\n- **NoOpSecurityAnalyzer:** always returns UNKNOWN. The page suggests it for development, trusted environments, or cases where confirmation mode handles everything.\n\n**Confirmation policies**\n- **AlwaysConfirm:** confirms every action.\n- **NeverConfirm:** never requires confirmation.\n- **ConfirmRisky:** configurable and the default. Actions at or above `threshold` require confirmation. The `confirm_unknown` flag controls UNKNOWN risk.\n\n**How actions are gated**\nThe agent validates actions before execution. When confirmation is required, the conversation pauses for user approval.\n\n**Defaults**\n- Policy: ConfirmRisky\n- `threshold`: HIGH\n- `confirm_unknown`: True (so UNKNOWN actions require confirmation)\n- The threshold cannot be set to UNKNOWN.\n- The security analyzer is optional and can be disabled for trusted environments.\n\n**What is rated vs. blocked**\n- Every action is rated LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or UNKNOWN.\n- With default settings, HIGH and UNKNOWN actions pause for confirmation. LOW and MEDIUM proceed.\n- The page describes pausing for confirmation, not outright blocking. It also mentions MCP tool hints as an input to risk assessment but gives no detail.\n\n**Limits acknowledged**\nThe page has no explicit limitations section. Its design implies some constraints:\n- Under the LLM analyzer, risk ratings come from the model's own in-context reasoning, and the page does not describe independent verification.\n- UNKNOWN actions can proceed without confirmation if `confirm_unknown` is set to False.\n- Security is optional and can be turned off."}]},"uuid":"e47e3255-7409-432f-b634-1cfab573f62c","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:27.799Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":12558,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Security Architecture Summary\n\n**Purpose:** The system assesses agent actions for risk before execution. It uses pluggable analyzers and confirmation policies to decide when user approval is needed. It has four stated responsibilities: risk assessment, confirmation policy, action validation, and an audit trail of security decisions in event history.\n\n**Risk levels**\n- **LOW:** read-only, no state changes\n- **MEDIUM:** modifies user data\n- **HIGH:** dangerous operations such as deletion, system commands, or privilege escalation\n- **UNKNOWN:** not analyzed or indeterminate\n\n**Security analyzers**\n- **LLMSecurityAnalyzer** (inline assessment): A required `security_risk` parameter is added to non-read-only tool schemas. The LLM supplies a risk level with each tool call, and the analyzer returns it. The page states \"Risk assessment happens inline—no separate analysis step,\" so there are no extra LLM calls.\n- **NoOpSecurityAnalyzer:** always returns UNKNOWN. The page suggests it for development, trusted environments, or cases where confirmation mode handles everything.\n\n**Confirmation policies**\n- **AlwaysConfirm:** confirms every action.\n- **NeverConfirm:** never requires confirmation.\n- **ConfirmRisky:** configurable and the default. Actions at or above `threshold` require confirmation. The `confirm_unknown` flag controls UNKNOWN risk.\n\n**How actions are gated**\nThe agent validates actions before execution. When confirmation is required, the conversation pauses for user approval.\n\n**Defaults**\n- Policy: ConfirmRisky\n- `threshold`: HIGH\n- `confirm_unknown`: True (so UNKNOWN actions require confirmation)\n- The threshold cannot be set to UNKNOWN.\n- The security analyzer is optional and can be disabled for trusted environments.\n\n**What is rated vs. blocked**\n- Every action is rated LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH, or UNKNOWN.\n- With default settings, HIGH and UNKNOWN actions pause for confirmation. LOW and MEDIUM proceed.\n- The page describes pausing for confirmation, not outright blocking. It also mentions MCP tool hints as an input to risk assessment but gives no detail.\n\n**Limits acknowledged**\nThe page has no explicit limitations section. Its design implies some constraints:\n- Under the LLM analyzer, risk ratings come from the model's own in-context reasoning, and the page does not describe independent verification.\n- UNKNOWN actions can proceed without confirmation if `confirm_unknown` is set to False.\n- Security is optional and can be turned off.","durationMs":8421,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/security"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"46008d60-8330-42a8-8253-e967640d5237","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"b12cfee3-220e-4fbe-a004-be6e04f88c46","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"f5b6fb84-e2a1-484f-9c7c-ab32c73e4b22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017LgToRNnHoE5h81hLBy8XN","type":"tool_result","content":"**Trigger**\n- **Automatic:** Each agent step, the agent calls `condense(view)`. Condensation fires when a threshold is exceeded, such as event count above `max_size` (default 120). The stated purpose is to \"Proactively keep context within limits.\"\n- **Manual:** A `CondensationRequest` event is added to history, typically after an LLM context-window error. The page describes this as forcing compression when the context limit has been exceeded.\n\n**Strategies**\n- **NoOpCondenser:** Passes the view through unchanged.\n- **LLMSummarizingCondenser:** Keeps the first `keep_first` events (default 4) and summarizes the middle with a dedicated LLM, which can be a cheaper model than the reasoning model.\n- **Rolling window (base pattern):** Keeps head and tail events and summarizes the middle. After condensation, the view targets `max_size // 2` events (default 60). The page describes head-and-tail retention rather than pure truncation.\n- **PipelineCondenser:** Chains stages, such as removing old events, then summarizing, then truncating.\n\n**What is preserved**\n- The initial events (described as usually system prompts)\n- Recent tail events, which carry the current context\n- A summary standing in for the forgotten middle\n\n**Interaction with the event log**\n- A `Condensation` event records `forgotten_event_ids`, the `summary`, and a `summary_offset` for where the summary is inserted. It is a regular event with an id, timestamp, and source.\n- When a condenser returns a `Condensation` instead of a `View`, the agent appends it to the log and ends that step early.\n- On the next step, `View.from_events()` filters out the forgotten events and inserts the summary. The LLM sees the condensed view.\n- The page doesn't say whether forgotten events are deleted from storage or only hidden from the view, and it doesn't describe persistence details.\n\n**Trade-offs**\nThe page doesn't include a dedicated trade-offs section. Implied costs include:\n- The summary is a compressed representation, so detail from the middle can be lost.\n- Summarization requires an extra LLM call, though a cheaper model can be used.\n- Condensation costs an early step exit, so the agent's turn is deferred."}]},"uuid":"844d7355-8066-4b09-9ac9-a296cec446a1","timestamp":"2026-11-15T13:21:30.199Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":15171,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Trigger**\n- **Automatic:** Each agent step, the agent calls `condense(view)`. Condensation fires when a threshold is exceeded, such as event count above `max_size` (default 120). The stated purpose is to \"Proactively keep context within limits.\"\n- **Manual:** A `CondensationRequest` event is added to history, typically after an LLM context-window error. The page describes this as forcing compression when the context limit has been exceeded.\n\n**Strategies**\n- **NoOpCondenser:** Passes the view through unchanged.\n- **LLMSummarizingCondenser:** Keeps the first `keep_first` events (default 4) and summarizes the middle with a dedicated LLM, which can be a cheaper model than the reasoning model.\n- **Rolling window (base pattern):** Keeps head and tail events and summarizes the middle. After condensation, the view targets `max_size // 2` events (default 60). The page describes head-and-tail retention rather than pure truncation.\n- **PipelineCondenser:** Chains stages, such as removing old events, then summarizing, then truncating.\n\n**What is preserved**\n- The initial events (described as usually system prompts)\n- Recent tail events, which carry the current context\n- A summary standing in for the forgotten middle\n\n**Interaction with the event log**\n- A `Condensation` event records `forgotten_event_ids`, the `summary`, and a `summary_offset` for where the summary is inserted. It is a regular event with an id, timestamp, and source.\n- When a condenser returns a `Condensation` instead of a `View`, the agent appends it to the log and ends that step early.\n- On the next step, `View.from_events()` filters out the forgotten events and inserts the summary. The LLM sees the condensed view.\n- The page doesn't say whether forgotten events are deleted from storage or only hidden from the view, and it doesn't describe persistence details.\n\n**Trade-offs**\nThe page doesn't include a dedicated trade-offs section. Implied costs include:\n- The summary is a compressed representation, so detail from the middle can be lost.\n- Summarization requires an extra LLM call, though a cheaper model can be used.\n- Condensation costs an early step exit, so the agent's turn is deferred.","durationMs":9791,"url":"https://docs.openhands.dev/sdk/arch/condenser"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b12cfee3-220e-4fbe-a004-be6e04f88c46","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
