{"parentUuid":"19bc6e67-60a0-415a-99fe-d90ccb486a76","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4rUNYLcyME28v2Uaqdu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LaCcx8yW9FpG7jAyRNJ8G7","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://claude.com/blog/context-management","prompt":"Summarize this post in detail: the problem it addresses, the features/techniques introduced (e.g. context editing, memory tool, compaction, tool-result clearing, thresholds), how each works, concrete numbers/benchmarks claimed, recommended usage patterns, and limitations or caveats. Quote key claims briefly."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21,"cache_read_input_tokens":413037,"output_tokens":189,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":189,"cache_read_input_tokens":413037,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":980,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LaCcx8yW9FpG7jAyRNJ8G7":{"url":"https://claude.com/blog/context-management","prompt":"Summarize this post in detail: the problem it addresses, the features/techniques introduced (e.g. context editing, memory tool, compaction, tool-result clearing, thresholds), how each works, concrete numbers/benchmarks claimed, recommended usage patterns, and limitations or caveats. Quote key claims briefly."}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch4rUN5YTRgvTzhqZFoZU","requestRef":"e27f5296-56e7-4639-8e3d-fcd760ab8975","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"e0bcac18-25b2-4f82-b8cd-ecd6e9215e3b","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:14:54.715Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"e0bcac18-25b2-4f82-b8cd-ecd6e9215e3b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"408100c2-056c-447a-af07-38c31b362777","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LaCcx8yW9FpG7jAyRNJ8G7","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary: Context Management on the Claude Developer Platform\n\n**Problem addressed:** Production agents handle more complex tasks and generate more tool results, so they exhaust their context windows. Developers must either truncate agent transcripts or accept degraded performance. The article's heading frames it this way: \"Context windows have limits, but real work doesn't.\"\n\n**Techniques introduced**\n\n1. **Context editing.** This clears stale content from the context window as the agent runs. Per the article, it \"automatically clears stale tool calls and results from within the context window when approaching token limits.\" It keeps the conversation flow intact, so agents can run longer without manual intervention. The article also claims it improves model focus, since Claude concentrates on relevant context.\n\n2. **Memory tool.** This lets Claude store and consult information outside the context window using a file-based system. Claude can \"create, read, update, and delete files in a dedicated memory directory stored in your infrastructure\" that persists across conversations. Knowledge can accumulate across sessions, and project state can be maintained without keeping everything in context. The article notes that \"The memory tool operates entirely client-side through tool calls,\" and that developers control the storage backend.\n\n3. **Built-in context awareness (Claude Sonnet 4.5).** The model tracks its available tokens throughout a conversation, which the article says helps it manage context more effectively.\n\nThe two main features work together: context editing removes stale results, and memory preserves key learnings for later sessions.\n\n**Concrete numbers (internal evaluation, agentic search)**\n- Memory tool plus context editing: \"improved performance by 39% over baseline.\"\n- Context editing alone: a 29% improvement.\n- 100-turn web search evaluation: context editing \"enabled agents to complete workflows that would otherwise fail due to context exhaustion,\" while \"reducing token consumption by 84%.\"\n\n**Recommended usage patterns**\n- **Coding:** Clear old file reads and test results while memory keeps debugging insights and architectural decisions.\n- **Research:** Store key findings in memory and remove old search results.\n- **Data processing:** Keep intermediate results in memory and clear raw data so workflows don't exceed token limits.\n\n**Limitations and caveats**\n- The benchmark figures come from an internal Anthropic evaluation on agentic search. The article doesn't describe the methodology in detail or provide independent verification.\n- The features are in public beta and are available on the Claude Developer Platform, Amazon Bedrock, and Google Cloud Vertex AI.\n- The memory tool requires developers to build and operate the storage backend, so they retain responsibility for where data lives and how it persists.\n- The article gives no specific token thresholds. It refers only to \"approaching token limits.\"\n- The article does not discuss compaction (summarizing context) or tool-result clearing as separate techniques. Your question mentions both, but the article covers only context editing, which includes clearing stale tool results. For details on those, consult the linked context-editing documentation."}]},"uuid":"8754162f-c32c-482e-8892-2911e31ae110","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:15:04.980Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":547440,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Summary: Context Management on the Claude Developer Platform\n\n**Problem addressed:** Production agents handle more complex tasks and generate more tool results, so they exhaust their context windows. Developers must either truncate agent transcripts or accept degraded performance. The article's heading frames it this way: \"Context windows have limits, but real work doesn't.\"\n\n**Techniques introduced**\n\n1. **Context editing.** This clears stale content from the context window as the agent runs. Per the article, it \"automatically clears stale tool calls and results from within the context window when approaching token limits.\" It keeps the conversation flow intact, so agents can run longer without manual intervention. The article also claims it improves model focus, since Claude concentrates on relevant context.\n\n2. **Memory tool.** This lets Claude store and consult information outside the context window using a file-based system. Claude can \"create, read, update, and delete files in a dedicated memory directory stored in your infrastructure\" that persists across conversations. Knowledge can accumulate across sessions, and project state can be maintained without keeping everything in context. The article notes that \"The memory tool operates entirely client-side through tool calls,\" and that developers control the storage backend.\n\n3. **Built-in context awareness (Claude Sonnet 4.5).** The model tracks its available tokens throughout a conversation, which the article says helps it manage context more effectively.\n\nThe two main features work together: context editing removes stale results, and memory preserves key learnings for later sessions.\n\n**Concrete numbers (internal evaluation, agentic search)**\n- Memory tool plus context editing: \"improved performance by 39% over baseline.\"\n- Context editing alone: a 29% improvement.\n- 100-turn web search evaluation: context editing \"enabled agents to complete workflows that would otherwise fail due to context exhaustion,\" while \"reducing token consumption by 84%.\"\n\n**Recommended usage patterns**\n- **Coding:** Clear old file reads and test results while memory keeps debugging insights and architectural decisions.\n- **Research:** Store key findings in memory and remove old search results.\n- **Data processing:** Keep intermediate results in memory and clear raw data so workflows don't exceed token limits.\n\n**Limitations and caveats**\n- The benchmark figures come from an internal Anthropic evaluation on agentic search. The article doesn't describe the methodology in detail or provide independent verification.\n- The features are in public beta and are available on the Claude Developer Platform, Amazon Bedrock, and Google Cloud Vertex AI.\n- The memory tool requires developers to build and operate the storage backend, so they retain responsibility for where data lives and how it persists.\n- The article gives no specific token thresholds. It refers only to \"approaching token limits.\"\n- The article does not discuss compaction (summarizing context) or tool-result clearing as separate techniques. Your question mentions both, but the article covers only context editing, which includes clearing stale tool results. For details on those, consult the linked context-editing documentation.","durationMs":10018,"url":"https://claude.com/blog/context-management"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e0bcac18-25b2-4f82-b8cd-ecd6e9215e3b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
