{"parentUuid":"8754162f-c32c-482e-8892-2911e31ae110","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4rVKs8dnL9YE7tn4ozH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1168,"cache_read_input_tokens":413058,"output_tokens":2248,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1247},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1168,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2248,"cache_read_input_tokens":413058,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1168,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1168},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":14588,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch4rVKck8cQ2q5Y8ZZ8MY","requestRef":"ccc0a2fe-f373-491c-8c27-a74bd8db2ef3","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"b391775d-3338-4291-a065-a0cca63e8600","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:15:21.154Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"b391775d-3338-4291-a065-a0cca63e8600","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4rVKs8dnL9YE7tn4ozH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fetched the post through a page summarizer, so the quotes are paraphrase. It describes three features on the Claude Developer Platform: context editing, a memory tool, and context awareness in Sonnet 4.5. All of it is public beta. It never mentions compaction (summarizing) as a separate feature, so for that you'd need the linked docs.\n\n**What it says**\n- **Context editing:** this clears stale tool calls and results when approaching the token limit, with the conversation flow kept intact. It is deterministic, with no summarizer call.\n- **Memory tool:** a file-based create/read/update/delete directory that lives outside the window, runs client-side via tool calls, and persists across sessions. The developer owns the storage.\n- **Context awareness:** the model tracks its own remaining token budget.\n- **Together:** editing removes stale results and memory preserves the key learnings. For coding, clear old file reads and test output while memory keeps the debugging insights and architectural decisions.\n\n**Lessons for Nexus**\n- **Cheap clearing before summarizing.** Dropping stale tool results costs nothing and loses nothing the model can't re-fetch. Summarizing costs a call and is lossy. Our auto-compaction escalates through stronger passes, but I haven't checked whether the first pass is plain tool-result clearing. OpenHands' condenser is also a pipeline of stages.\n- **Clearing must not break the evidence chain.** This is where we differ from the post. A cleared result must stay retrievable from our evidence store, or the SI-1 rule (a reviewer ACCEPT needs verifier evidence) and the receipts could point at output the model can no longer see. I haven't verified that ours behaves this way.\n- **Keep what gates completion.** This is the same open gap as `compaction-03`: a post-compaction check that every gating requirement survived. The post's \"memory keeps the decisions\" idea maps onto our requirements ledger and `manage_knowledge`.\n- **Show the model its remaining budget.** We log `context_admission` and `token_count` for operators. I don't know whether the model sees its remaining budget. It's an option, but a risky one for local models, where the context limit is often an unreliable estimate.\n\n**Treat the numbers with care**\n- **Claimed gains:** +39% (memory plus editing), +29% (editing alone), and 84% fewer tokens on a 100-turn web-search task.\n- **Evidence quality:** it is an internal Anthropic evaluation on agentic search, with no methodology and no independent check. It also gives no token thresholds.\n- **What we'd need:** a measured test of our own, comparing clearing on and off on long-horizon CERI scenarios.\n\nThe v1.2.1 CX (context) units are the natural home for any of this. I haven't re-read them for this answer, and I haven't changed anything. The cheapest first step would be a read-only audit of our compaction passes against the first two points above.\n\nThe monitor on the `main` CI run for `181c3833` is still running, and #541 is still waiting on its checks."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1168,"cache_read_input_tokens":413058,"output_tokens":2248,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1247},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1168,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2248,"cache_read_input_tokens":413058,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1168,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1168},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations