eRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"c22c8e32-f5d5-4015-8452-328cc90525af","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:23:06.183Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"c22c8e32-f5d5-4015-8452-328cc90525af","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"93db3711-c191-4104-9d0a-da11f73c475a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QvQwZZebdxF32JD3bALf8U","type":"tool_result","content":"This web page is a landing page for the **2026 Agentic Coding Trends Report** from Anthropic. It doesn't include the report's full text, so I can't summarize its findings, statistics, or recommendations in detail.\n\n**Main themes (as described on the page)**\n- Software development is moving from writing code by hand to orchestrating AI agents that write code. The page puts it this way: \"Software development is shifting from writing code to orchestrating agents that write code.\"\n- Engineering leaders are struggling to move from early experiments to organization-wide adoption while balancing productivity against oversight, quality, and security.\n\n**Topics the report covers (named but not explained)**\n- Eight trends, of which four are named: shifting engineering roles, multi-agent coordination, human-AI collaboration patterns, and scaling agentic coding beyond engineering teams.\n- Where engineering teams see the greatest productivity gains today.\n- Why effective AI collaboration still requires active human judgment.\n- A diagram of a new software development lifecycle.\n- How non-technical teams build tools without engineering support.\n\n**Case studies:** Rakuten, CRED, TELUS, and Zapier are named, with no details about their results.\n\n**Key claims and statistics:** None. The page contains no quantitative data.\n\n**Recommendations for engineering teams or tool builders:** None are stated on the page.\n\n**Caveats**\n- This is a marketing or lead-generation page. Access requires submitting a form, and the page links to Anthropic's privacy policy.\n- The page cites no methodology, sample size, or data sources, so the trends can't be evaluated from this content alone.\n- The publisher is Anthropic, a vendor of agentic coding tools, so the report likely presents a favorable view of agentic coding. Its claims should be checked against the full report and independent sources.\n\nTo summarize the actual findings, you would need the full report, which the page says is available after submitting the form."}]},"uuid":"4afd5c4a-0a91-4530-9b15-1b9d7e224279","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:23:13.158Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":33912,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"This web page is a landing page for the **2026 Agentic Coding Trends Report** from Anthropic. It doesn't include the report's full text, so I can't summarize its findings, statistics, or recommendations in detail.\n\n**Main themes (as described on the page)**\n- Software development is moving from writing code by hand to orchestrating AI agents that write code. The page puts it this way: \"Software development is shifting from writing code to orchestrating agents that write code.\"\n- Engineering leaders are struggling to move from early experiments to organization-wide adoption while balancing productivity against oversight, quality, and security.\n\n**Topics the report covers (named but not explained)**\n- Eight trends, of which four are named: shifting engineering roles, multi-agent coordination, human-AI collaboration patterns, and scaling agentic coding beyond engineering teams.\n- Where engineering teams see the greatest productivity gains today.\n- Why effective AI collaboration still requires active human judgment.\n- A diagram of a new software development lifecycle.\n- How non-technical teams build tools without engineering support.\n\n**Case studies:** Rakuten, CRED, TELUS, and Zapier are named, with no details about their results.\n\n**Key claims and statistics:** None. The page contains no quantitative data.\n\n**Recommendations for engineering teams or tool builders:** None are stated on the page.\n\n**Caveats**\n- This is a marketing or lead-generation page. Access requires submitting a form, and the page links to Anthropic's privacy policy.\n- The page cites no methodology, sample size, or data sources, so the trends can't be evaluated from this content alone.\n- The publisher is Anthropic, a vendor of agentic coding tools, so the report likely presents a favorable view of agentic coding. Its claims should be checked against the full report and independent sources.\n\nTo summarize the actual findings, you would need the full report, which the page says is available after submitting the form.","durationMs":6680,"url":"https://resources.anthropic.com/2026-agentic-coding-trends-report"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c22c8e32-f5d5-4015-8452-328cc90525af","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
