{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","prNumber":542,"prUrl":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/542","prRepository":"mikeholownych/nexus-code","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:24:04.550Z"}
{"parentUuid":"b312e26b-5c0b-4829-9057-54a2059b8e26","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"93db3711-c191-4104-9d0a-da11f73c475a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011cxqpNJnCzQ9fY3kDqR2PJ","type":"tool_result","content":"The page doesn't list all eight trends. It says the report identifies eight trends across three categories (foundation, capability, and impact), but it only summarizes six takeaways, and it doesn't map them to the eight.\n\n**Takeaways (paraphrased)**\n\n1. **SDLC restructuring:** Agent-driven implementation, automated testing, and inline documentation shorten cycles, and engineers shift toward orchestrating agents and making architectural decisions. The page says cycle time collapses \"from weeks to hours.\"\n2. **Single agents become coordinated teams:** An orchestrator directs specialized sub-agents working in parallel, which the page links to the orchestrator-workers pattern.\n3. **Long-running agents:** Task horizons are growing \"from minutes to days or weeks,\" with agents maintaining coherent state across many work sessions.\n4. **Human oversight through collaboration:** Engineers use AI in roughly 60% of their work but can fully delegate only 0–20% of tasks. Agents learn when to ask for help, and teams use escalation systems for boundary cases.\n5. **Expansion beyond engineering:** Product managers, designers, and marketers use coding agents for prototyping, data analysis, and workflow automation.\n6. **Security as a first-class concern:** Agents that can build can also introduce vulnerabilities, so the page calls for security-first architecture, automated code review, and oversight designed for agent-generated code at scale.\n\n**Statistics and case studies**\n\n- Roughly 60% of engineers' work involves AI.\n- 0–20% of tasks are fully delegated.\n- The page contains no company case studies or other metrics.\n\n**Closing priorities:** Not included on the page.\n\n**Caveats:** The page states none. It also doesn't say whether the 60% and 0–20% figures come from a survey or how they were measured.\n\nFor the full list of eight trends, closing priorities, and any caveats, check the original PDF: https://resources.anthropic.com/hubfs/2026%20Agentic%20Coding%20Trends%20Report.pdf"}]},"uuid":"bd7b5f3e-f41c-4ed8-8bab-d99d89c8ef29","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:24:13.381Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":84921,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"The page doesn't list all eight trends. It says the report identifies eight trends across three categories (foundation, capability, and impact), but it only summarizes six takeaways, and it doesn't map them to the eight.\n\n**Takeaways (paraphrased)**\n\n1. **SDLC restructuring:** Agent-driven implementation, automated testing, and inline documentation shorten cycles, and engineers shift toward orchestrating agents and making architectural decisions. The page says cycle time collapses \"from weeks to hours.\"\n2. **Single agents become coordinated teams:** An orchestrator directs specialized sub-agents working in parallel, which the page links to the orchestrator-workers pattern.\n3. **Long-running agents:** Task horizons are growing \"from minutes to days or weeks,\" with agents maintaining coherent state across many work sessions.\n4. **Human oversight through collaboration:** Engineers use AI in roughly 60% of their work but can fully delegate only 0–20% of tasks. Agents learn when to ask for help, and teams use escalation systems for boundary cases.\n5. **Expansion beyond engineering:** Product managers, designers, and marketers use coding agents for prototyping, data analysis, and workflow automation.\n6. **Security as a first-class concern:** Agents that can build can also introduce vulnerabilities, so the page calls for security-first architecture, automated code review, and oversight designed for agent-generated code at scale.\n\n**Statistics and case studies**\n\n- Roughly 60% of engineers' work involves AI.\n- 0–20% of tasks are fully delegated.\n- The page contains no company case studies or other metrics.\n\n**Closing priorities:** Not included on the page.\n\n**Caveats:** The page states none. It also doesn't say whether the 60% and 0–20% figures come from a survey or how they were measured.\n\nFor the full list of eight trends, closing priorities, and any caveats, check the original PDF: https://resources.anthropic.com/hubfs/2026%20Agentic%20Coding%20Trends%20Report.pdf","durationMs":9607,"url":"https://handsonai.info/resources/anthropic-coding-trends/"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b312e26b-5c0b-4829-9057-54a2059b8e26","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"fb8a5245-bc10-458b-a018-84dac4d7ab72","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"93db3711-c191-4104-9d0a-da11f73c475a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wnj3waUhcvMzvKsDYRQVJD","type":"tool_result","content":"**The eight trends (per the article's account of Anthropic's report)**\n\n1. **Tectonic shift in the SDLC:** Engineering roles move toward agent supervision, system design, and output review. Agents handle much of the writing, testing, debugging, and documentation, and humans stay responsible for judgment and verification.\n2. **Agents become team players:** Teams move from single coding agents to specialised agents working in parallel under an orchestrator, which requires better task breakdown and coordination.\n3. **Agents go end-to-end:** Agents shift from short tasks to multi-hour or multi-day work, handling planning, error recovery, and project context, with humans checking progress at key points. Full application builds and technical debt reduction become more feasible.\n4. **Agents learn when to ask for help:** Newer systems flag uncertainty and risk and request human input at key decision points. Agents also review AI-generated code, so human review can focus on judgment-heavy calls.\n5. **Agents spread beyond software engineers:** Support extends to legacy and niche languages such as COBOL and Fortran, and to non-traditional developers in security, operations, design, and data roles.\n6. **More code, shorter timelines:** Faster delivery makes more projects practical. The article states that \"Work that once took weeks can be done in days,\" and that lower delivery effort reduces cost per feature.\n7. **Non-engineers embrace agentic coding:** Sales, legal, marketing, and operations teams build their own small tools and automations without waiting on engineering queues.\n8. **Agents help defenders and attackers scale:** Agents improve deeper code reviews and hardening checks, but also let attackers \"speed up reconnaissance and exploit development.\" The article's implied response is building security controls in early and using automated defenses.\n\n**Statistics and case-study results:** The article contains none. Its trend claims are qualitative, and it does not cite company names, metrics, or figures from Anthropic's report. (The \"20%\" cost figure and Microsoft data appear only in unrelated \"Your next read\" teasers, so I excluded them.)\n\n**Closing priorities and recommendations:** The article gives no explicit recommendations of its own. Its closing section stresses that engineers should focus on \"direction, judgment, and oversight,\" with more time spent on orchestration, review, and system design. It also cites industry analysis favoring engineers who can scope problems, supervise agent output, and make high-stakes technical calls.\n\n**Criticisms and caveats:** The author raises no explicit criticism of the trends. The article hedges throughout, using phrases like \"expected to\" and \"according to Anthropic,\" and notes that developers currently delegate only a limited share of work fully to AI. It also promotes Tessl, an agent enablement platform, which the author's own company operates."}]},"uuid":"5696a198-0fbe-41d4-a85e-f58081058a98","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:24:17.027Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":286102,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**The eight trends (per the article's account of Anthropic's report)**\n\n1. **Tectonic shift in the SDLC:** Engineering roles move toward agent supervision, system design, and output review. Agents handle much of the writing, testing, debugging, and documentation, and humans stay responsible for judgment and verification.\n2. **Agents become team players:** Teams move from single coding agents to specialised agents working in parallel under an orchestrator, which requires better task breakdown and coordination.\n3. **Agents go end-to-end:** Agents shift from short tasks to multi-hour or multi-day work, handling planning, error recovery, and project context, with humans checking progress at key points. Full application builds and technical debt reduction become more feasible.\n4. **Agents learn when to ask for help:** Newer systems flag uncertainty and risk and request human input at key decision points. Agents also review AI-generated code, so human review can focus on judgment-heavy calls.\n5. **Agents spread beyond software engineers:** Support extends to legacy and niche languages such as COBOL and Fortran, and to non-traditional developers in security, operations, design, and data roles.\n6. **More code, shorter timelines:** Faster delivery makes more projects practical. The article states that \"Work that once took weeks can be done in days,\" and that lower delivery effort reduces cost per feature.\n7. **Non-engineers embrace agentic coding:** Sales, legal, marketing, and operations teams build their own small tools and automations without waiting on engineering queues.\n8. **Agents help defenders and attackers scale:** Agents improve deeper code reviews and hardening checks, but also let attackers \"speed up reconnaissance and exploit development.\" The article's implied response is building security controls in early and using automated defenses.\n\n**Statistics and case-study results:** The article contains none. Its trend claims are qualitative, and it does not cite company names, metrics, or figures from Anthropic's report. (The \"20%\" cost figure and Microsoft data appear only in unrelated \"Your next read\" teasers, so I excluded them.)\n\n**Closing priorities and recommendations:** The article gives no explicit recommendations of its own. Its closing section stresses that engineers should focus on \"direction, judgment, and oversight,\" with more time spent on orchestration, review, and system design. It also cites industry analysis favoring engineers who can scope problems, supervise agent output, and make high-stakes technical calls.\n\n**Criticisms and caveats:** The author raises no explicit criticism of the trends. The article hedges throughout, using phrases like \"expected to\" and \"according to Anthropic,\" and notes that developers currently delegate only a limited share of work fully to AI. It also promotes Tessl, an agent enablement platform, which the author's own company operates.","durationMs":13392,"url":"https://tessl.io/blog/8-trends-shaping-software-engineering-in-2026-according-to-anthropics-agentic-coding-report/"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fb8a5245-bc10-458b-a018-84dac4d7ab72","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
