{"parentUuid":"2d5e08d0-74ab-4c19-ba12-5b102b39dec2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4sR6agPko4qjGp4Rr4U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've now read all 18 pages of the [report PDF](https://resources.anthropic.com/hubfs/2026%20Agentic%20Coding%20Trends%20Report.pdf). It confirms my earlier summary-based answer with some corrections, listed at the end.\n\n**What the report says**\n- **Structure:** eight trends in three groups (foundation, capability, impact), then four priorities for 2026. The foreword says these are \"what we're seeing with customers today, not certainties about tomorrow\".\n- **The delegation figure:** developers use AI in roughly 60% of their work but report being able to \"fully delegate\" only 0–20% of tasks. It comes from Anthropic's Societal Impacts research and is self-reported.\n- **Trend 3, the long-running case:** Rakuten had Claude Code implement a method in a 12.5-million-line open-source library in seven hours of autonomous work in a single run, at 99.9% numerical accuracy compared to the reference method.\n- **Trend 4, human oversight:** it predicts \"agentic quality control becomes standard\", with AI agents reviewing large-scale AI-generated output, and agents that learn \"when to ask for help\". It also says engineers delegate tasks that are \"easily verifiable\", where they can \"sniff-check on correctness\", and keep the design-dependent ones.\n- **Other case-study numbers, all vendor-chosen:** TELUS says shipping code 30% faster and over 500,000 hours saved. Fountain says 50% faster screening and 40% quicker onboarding. CRED says execution speed doubled. About 27% of AI-assisted work is tasks that wouldn't otherwise have been done.\n- **Priorities:** multi-agent coordination, scaling human-agent oversight through AI-automated review, extending agentic coding beyond engineering, and building security in from the start.\n\n**Lessons for Nexus**\n- **The delegation boundary is verifiability.** The report's own evidence says people hand off what they can check cheaply. So the product lever is making more work cheaply checkable: typed requirements, verifier evidence and receipts. That is the same direction as our SI-1 rule, where a reviewer ACCEPT without deterministic evidence is REJECT. The report treats AI review as how oversight scales; our line is stricter, that AI review alone isn't evidence.\n- **The flagship long-run result had a reference to compare against.** Rakuten's task succeeded with a reference implementation to check against. Our differential-verification unit (DV-4, a declared reference) was deferred in v1.2.0. That makes it the natural enabler for long autonomous runs, though I haven't re-read its plan entry.\n- **Multi-agent needs infrastructure the report names:** a view of concurrent agent sessions and version-control workflows for simultaneous contributions. Our `git.isolate` rule (SI-8) covers the second. A status view of concurrent sessions is a candidate for the v1.3 work; I haven't checked whether it's already planned.\n- **Security by design** matches the v1.2.0 verification-path guard and language-server containment.\n- **Skip trends 5–7.** Non-engineers building tools isn't our target.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The report is a vendor document, with no methodology section.\n- The case-study figures are customer-reported, and the 60% and 0–20% numbers are self-reported.\n- I'd use it for framing, not as evidence about Nexus.\n\n**Corrections to my earlier answer:** the report's Trend 8 is dual-use risk (defence and attack, security-first architecture). Trend 4 is about oversight scaling by AI review, and it's the report's own phrase that agents \"learn when to ask for help\". The summaries had also left out the Rakuten reference-comparison detail, which I think is the most useful single fact in it.\n\nI haven't changed anything, and I haven't rechecked CI since my last status."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2970,"cache_read_input_tokens":466226,"output_tokens":2745,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1466},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2970,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2745,"cache_read_input_tokens":466226,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2970},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch4sR6F5Q6BGwzq13U4eZ","requestRef":"ecabbd37-7d41-4ede-a0be-0acde90ff4e1","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"4de4a1fb-5741-49b1-b39d-b3a80503c60f","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:27:35.716Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
