{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","prNumber":542,"prUrl":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/542","prRepository":"mikeholownych/nexus-code","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:10.167Z"}
{"parentUuid":"304fd782-788d-4a1f-87db-4747701e4039","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"55e5c103-f7a2-454f-8998-dfa743d591d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011VPG4giZq2V26VuovVsd3A","type":"tool_result","content":"# Structure of the Ponytail Rules File\n\n**Title and intro:** \"Ponytail, lazy senior dev mode.\" It sets the persona: \"You are a lazy senior developer.\" It also states the core idea: \"The best code is the code never written.\"\n\n**Reply ending:** Every reply ends with one or two lines covering what was skipped or not checked, and any risk the user must know.\n\n**Section: Before you write.** Read the task and the code it touches, then list every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config, exports). Check what the change could break for users. Scope is defined as this work, and extra features are excluded.\n\n**Section: The smallest complete change.** This holds the ladder of questions, a numbered sequence where the first option that fully works wins:\n\n1. Does it need to exist?\n2. Already in this codebase?\n3. Standard library or platform feature?\n4. Installed dependency?\n5. Can it be one line a reader gets at a glance?\n6. Otherwise, the minimum code that works.\n\nBelow the ladder, bulleted rules cover:\n- **Scope of laziness:** \"Be lazy about the solution, never about the change itself.\"\n- **Bug fixes:** grep every caller first, then fix the root cause once.\n- **Ties:** between equal-size options, pick the one correct on edge cases.\n- **Tests:** \"Lazy code without its check is unfinished.\" Non-trivial logic gets one small test or assert-based self-check. Trivial changes need none. This is the test rule.\n- **Shortcut convention:** a known limit gets a comment in the form `shortcut: <the limit>, <when to upgrade>`.\n\n**Guardrails:** A \"Never cut\" line lists what laziness must not remove: validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security, accessibility, and hardware calibration.\n\n**Closing note:** The file says it also applies to agents working on the ponytail repo itself.\n\n**Intensity levels:** The file has no intensity levels or tiers.\n\n## Style\n\nThe file is terse and imperative, using short sentences, bullets, and a numbered ladder. Its tone is dry and slightly playful, as in \"Deletion beats addition.\" Rules are mostly stated positively (\"Use it,\" \"Take the first option\"), but prohibitions are frequent and direct, often introduced with \"Never\" or \"No\" (\"No abstraction, wrapper...\"; \"Never add a dependency...\"). Both forms carry the same firmness."}]},"uuid":"493e5c9c-55ef-4b83-84cf-c5a8cc62d7c4","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:19.060Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":2721,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Structure of the Ponytail Rules File\n\n**Title and intro:** \"Ponytail, lazy senior dev mode.\" It sets the persona: \"You are a lazy senior developer.\" It also states the core idea: \"The best code is the code never written.\"\n\n**Reply ending:** Every reply ends with one or two lines covering what was skipped or not checked, and any risk the user must know.\n\n**Section: Before you write.** Read the task and the code it touches, then list every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config, exports). Check what the change could break for users. Scope is defined as this work, and extra features are excluded.\n\n**Section: The smallest complete change.** This holds the ladder of questions, a numbered sequence where the first option that fully works wins:\n\n1. Does it need to exist?\n2. Already in this codebase?\n3. Standard library or platform feature?\n4. Installed dependency?\n5. Can it be one line a reader gets at a glance?\n6. Otherwise, the minimum code that works.\n\nBelow the ladder, bulleted rules cover:\n- **Scope of laziness:** \"Be lazy about the solution, never about the change itself.\"\n- **Bug fixes:** grep every caller first, then fix the root cause once.\n- **Ties:** between equal-size options, pick the one correct on edge cases.\n- **Tests:** \"Lazy code without its check is unfinished.\" Non-trivial logic gets one small test or assert-based self-check. Trivial changes need none. This is the test rule.\n- **Shortcut convention:** a known limit gets a comment in the form `shortcut: <the limit>, <when to upgrade>`.\n\n**Guardrails:** A \"Never cut\" line lists what laziness must not remove: validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security, accessibility, and hardware calibration.\n\n**Closing note:** The file says it also applies to agents working on the ponytail repo itself.\n\n**Intensity levels:** The file has no intensity levels or tiers.\n\n## Style\n\nThe file is terse and imperative, using short sentences, bullets, and a numbered ladder. Its tone is dry and slightly playful, as in \"Deletion beats addition.\" Rules are mostly stated positively (\"Use it,\" \"Take the first option\"), but prohibitions are frequent and direct, often introduced with \"Never\" or \"No\" (\"No abstraction, wrapper...\"; \"Never add a dependency...\"). Both forms carry the same firmness.","durationMs":10041,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail/main/AGENTS.md"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"304fd782-788d-4a1f-87db-4747701e4039","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","hash":"0cd3723ceecebc5c43cb928b2fb57cc82854280d6c521453a4848b5934cf9af1","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011VPG4giZq2V26VuovVsd3A","type":"tool_result","content":"# Structure of the Ponytail Rules File\n\n**Title and intro:** \"Ponytail, lazy senior dev mode.\" It sets the persona: \"You are a lazy senior developer.\" It also states the core idea: \"The best code is the code never written.\"\n\n**Reply ending:** Every reply ends with one or two lines covering what was skipped or not checked, and any risk the user must know.\n\n**Section: Before you write.** Read the task and the code it touches, then list every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config, exports). Check what the change could break for users. Scope is defined as this work, and extra features are excluded.\n\n**Section: The smallest complete change.** This holds the ladder of questions, a numbered sequence where the first option that fully works wins:\n\n1. Does it need to exist?\n2. Already in this codebase?\n3. Standard library or platform feature?\n4. Installed dependency?\n5. Can it be one line a reader gets at a glance?\n6. Otherwise, the minimum code that works.\n\nBelow the ladder, bulleted rules cover:\n- **Scope of laziness:** \"Be lazy about the solution, never about the change itself.\"\n- **Bug fixes:** grep every caller first, then fix the root cause once.\n- **Ties:** between equal-size options, pick the one correct on edge cases.\n- **Tests:** \"Lazy code without its check is unfinished.\" Non-trivial logic gets one small test or assert-based self-check. Trivial changes need none. This is the test rule.\n- **Shortcut convention:** a known limit gets a comment in the form `shortcut: <the limit>, <when to upgrade>`.\n\n**Guardrails:** A \"Never cut\" line lists what laziness must not remove: validation at trust boundaries, error handling that prevents data loss, security, accessibility, and hardware calibration.\n\n**Closing note:** The file says it also applies to agents working on the ponytail repo itself.\n\n**Intensity levels:** The file has no intensity levels or tiers.\n\n## Style\n\nThe file is terse and imperative, using short sentences, bullets, and a numbered ladder. Its tone is dry and slightly playful, as in \"Deletion beats addition.\" Rules are mostly stated positively (\"Use it,\" \"Take the first option\"), but prohibitions are frequent and direct, often introduced with \"Never\" or \"No\" (\"No abstraction, wrapper...\"; \"Never add a dependency...\"). Both forms carry the same firmness."},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017txbfrtxEZPqp1bpvAG3za","type":"tool_result","content":"## Files in `benchmarks/`\n\nThe page shows a partial listing and includes a \"View all files\" link, so this may not be complete.\n\n- **Folders:** `agentic/`, `arms/`, `results/`\n- **Config and docs:** `.env.example`, `README.md`\n- **Scripts:** `behavior.js`, `behavior.yaml`, `correctness.js`, `loc.js`\n- **Promptfoo configs:** `promptfooconfig.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gemini.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gpt-newest.yaml`\n\n## Benchmark method (per the README)\n\nThe README describes two benchmarks.\n\n**1. Agentic benchmark (headline, 2026-10-07)**\n- **Setup:** A headless Claude Code session runs each task, with and without the skill. Results are scored on what the agent leaves behind.\n- **Task set:** 39 tasks.\n- **Runs:** 5 per task.\n- **Model:** Opus 5.5.\n- **Arms:** Ponytail 5, the previous Ponytail (v4.13), and no skill, run interleaved in one batch.\n- **Metrics:** lines of code, output tokens, cost, time, and \"hidden checks passed.\"\n- **Results vs. no skill:**\n\n| Arm | Lines of code | Output tokens | Cost | Time | Hidden checks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ponytail 5 | -53% | -45% | -26% | -41% | 97% |\n| Ponytail v4.13 | -48% | -43% | -16% | -38% | 96% |\n| No skill | — | — | — | — | 96% |\n\n- The README reports that 98% of risky logic ships with a test, versus 68% with no skill.\n- The full method and per-task tables are in `results/2026-10-07-agentic.md`, which I could not see.\n\n**2. Single-shot benchmark (previous Ponytail, 2026-06)**\n- **Task set:** Five everyday tasks: email validator, JS debounce, CSV sum, React countdown, and FastAPI rate limit.\n- **Runs:** 10 per cell, with the median reported.\n- **Models:** Claude Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus. The cost check was re-run at 30 reps with OpenAI and Gemini arms.\n- **Arms:** No skill (baseline), Caveman (a prose-compression skill), and Ponytail.\n- **Metrics:** Code lines, cost, and latency come from the API. Tokens are also reported.\n- **Calls:** Single-shot completions at default temperature.\n\n## How \"less code\" is measured\n\n- `loc.js` counts lines inside fenced code blocks in each response.\n- `correctness.js` is a gate. It extracts the code blocks and runs checks. The email, debounce, and CSV checks execute the code with Python or Node. The React and FastAPI checks use only keyword or structural regex.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A no-skill baseline arm.\n- A second skill (Caveman) as a comparison arm.\n- Interleaved runs in the agentic benchmark.\n- Medians across repeated runs in the single-shot benchmark.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\n- The README says the single-shot gap \"counts prose, not just code, and overstates the win.\"\n- Single-shot cost does not reflect multi-turn sessions. Session cost \"can come out higher or lower.\"\n- The React and FastAPI checks are not executed, so they verify structure rather than correctness.\n- The tasks are everyday tasks, not production-grade specs.\n- An agentic A/B test found Ponytail \"can also raise tool calls and cost on completion-forced tasks.\"\n- Independent benchmarks are linked as corroboration only. The README notes their numbers \"may shift between runs.\"\n- Reproducing the single-shot benchmark requires Node.js ≥ 22.22.0 and an Anthropic API key. The `--env-file ../.env` flag is required."}]}}
{"type":"api-request","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","id":"d7ad930c-f040-4e57-8aec-e7b18ecac903","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:20.723Z","version":"2.1.323","querySource":"repl_main_thread","shapeHash":"02245c82d4cb86350db44b7c9960d5f359fa3c3746ce766c103fc6f328b1a703","params":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","betas":["claude-code-20250219","oauth-2025-04-20","interleaved-thinking-2025-05-14","context-management-2025-06-27","prompt-caching-scope-2026-01-05","per-turn-control-2026-07-01","mid-conversation-tool-changes-2026-07-01","inline-tools-2026-09-15","advisor-tool-2026-03-01","thinking-token-count-2026-05-13","advanced-tool-use-2025-11-20","mid-conversation-system-clear-at-2026-08-21","effort-2025-11-24","thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01","thinking-display-updates-2026-08-18","extended-cache-ttl-2025-04-11","cache-diagnosis-2026-04-07","message-threads-2026-08-12"],"max_tokens":128000,"thinking":{"type":"adaptive","display":"updates"},"context_management":{"edits":[{"type":"clear_thinking_20251015","keep":"all"}]},"output_config":{"effort":"medium"}},"base":"f0e325ef-f607-450c-bd21-8e804b03f4b6","keep":891,"tail":["ec8152b4e3490b515abc491119a8024b63eabcc353083a3f977bbe8582d3ffd2","0cd3723ceecebc5c43cb928b2fb57cc82854280d6c521453a4848b5934cf9af1"],"cacheControl":[{"path":["system",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h","scope":"global"}},{"path":["system",2],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}},{"path":["messages",892,"content",1],"value":{"type":"ephemeral","ttl":"1h"}}],"requestDigest":"4548dd87dc157bf0d1860d2c6a72685ff46b06f076a6bc2c0a4b6291062e523f"}
{"parentUuid":"6b7e5edd-d67c-4786-8828-ddefe976bfab","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"55e5c103-f7a2-454f-8998-dfa743d591d3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017txbfrtxEZPqp1bpvAG3za","type":"tool_result","content":"## Files in `benchmarks/`\n\nThe page shows a partial listing and includes a \"View all files\" link, so this may not be complete.\n\n- **Folders:** `agentic/`, `arms/`, `results/`\n- **Config and docs:** `.env.example`, `README.md`\n- **Scripts:** `behavior.js`, `behavior.yaml`, `correctness.js`, `loc.js`\n- **Promptfoo configs:** `promptfooconfig.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gemini.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gpt-newest.yaml`\n\n## Benchmark method (per the README)\n\nThe README describes two benchmarks.\n\n**1. Agentic benchmark (headline, 2026-10-07)**\n- **Setup:** A headless Claude Code session runs each task, with and without the skill. Results are scored on what the agent leaves behind.\n- **Task set:** 39 tasks.\n- **Runs:** 5 per task.\n- **Model:** Opus 5.5.\n- **Arms:** Ponytail 5, the previous Ponytail (v4.13), and no skill, run interleaved in one batch.\n- **Metrics:** lines of code, output tokens, cost, time, and \"hidden checks passed.\"\n- **Results vs. no skill:**\n\n| Arm | Lines of code | Output tokens | Cost | Time | Hidden checks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ponytail 5 | -53% | -45% | -26% | -41% | 97% |\n| Ponytail v4.13 | -48% | -43% | -16% | -38% | 96% |\n| No skill | — | — | — | — | 96% |\n\n- The README reports that 98% of risky logic ships with a test, versus 68% with no skill.\n- The full method and per-task tables are in `results/2026-10-07-agentic.md`, which I could not see.\n\n**2. Single-shot benchmark (previous Ponytail, 2026-06)**\n- **Task set:** Five everyday tasks: email validator, JS debounce, CSV sum, React countdown, and FastAPI rate limit.\n- **Runs:** 10 per cell, with the median reported.\n- **Models:** Claude Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus. The cost check was re-run at 30 reps with OpenAI and Gemini arms.\n- **Arms:** No skill (baseline), Caveman (a prose-compression skill), and Ponytail.\n- **Metrics:** Code lines, cost, and latency come from the API. Tokens are also reported.\n- **Calls:** Single-shot completions at default temperature.\n\n## How \"less code\" is measured\n\n- `loc.js` counts lines inside fenced code blocks in each response.\n- `correctness.js` is a gate. It extracts the code blocks and runs checks. The email, debounce, and CSV checks execute the code with Python or Node. The React and FastAPI checks use only keyword or structural regex.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A no-skill baseline arm.\n- A second skill (Caveman) as a comparison arm.\n- Interleaved runs in the agentic benchmark.\n- Medians across repeated runs in the single-shot benchmark.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\n- The README says the single-shot gap \"counts prose, not just code, and overstates the win.\"\n- Single-shot cost does not reflect multi-turn sessions. Session cost \"can come out higher or lower.\"\n- The React and FastAPI checks are not executed, so they verify structure rather than correctness.\n- The tasks are everyday tasks, not production-grade specs.\n- An agentic A/B test found Ponytail \"can also raise tool calls and cost on completion-forced tasks.\"\n- Independent benchmarks are linked as corroboration only. The README notes their numbers \"may shift between runs.\"\n- Reproducing the single-shot benchmark requires Node.js ≥ 22.22.0 and an Anthropic API key. The `--env-file ../.env` flag is required."}]},"uuid":"37f0b3b3-3e92-4a80-ad38-b5f000f92b0c","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:20.191Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":239957,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Files in `benchmarks/`\n\nThe page shows a partial listing and includes a \"View all files\" link, so this may not be complete.\n\n- **Folders:** `agentic/`, `arms/`, `results/`\n- **Config and docs:** `.env.example`, `README.md`\n- **Scripts:** `behavior.js`, `behavior.yaml`, `correctness.js`, `loc.js`\n- **Promptfoo configs:** `promptfooconfig.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gemini.yaml`, `promptfooconfig.gpt-newest.yaml`\n\n## Benchmark method (per the README)\n\nThe README describes two benchmarks.\n\n**1. Agentic benchmark (headline, 2026-10-07)**\n- **Setup:** A headless Claude Code session runs each task, with and without the skill. Results are scored on what the agent leaves behind.\n- **Task set:** 39 tasks.\n- **Runs:** 5 per task.\n- **Model:** Opus 5.5.\n- **Arms:** Ponytail 5, the previous Ponytail (v4.13), and no skill, run interleaved in one batch.\n- **Metrics:** lines of code, output tokens, cost, time, and \"hidden checks passed.\"\n- **Results vs. no skill:**\n\n| Arm | Lines of code | Output tokens | Cost | Time | Hidden checks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ponytail 5 | -53% | -45% | -26% | -41% | 97% |\n| Ponytail v4.13 | -48% | -43% | -16% | -38% | 96% |\n| No skill | — | — | — | — | 96% |\n\n- The README reports that 98% of risky logic ships with a test, versus 68% with no skill.\n- The full method and per-task tables are in `results/2026-10-07-agentic.md`, which I could not see.\n\n**2. Single-shot benchmark (previous Ponytail, 2026-06)**\n- **Task set:** Five everyday tasks: email validator, JS debounce, CSV sum, React countdown, and FastAPI rate limit.\n- **Runs:** 10 per cell, with the median reported.\n- **Models:** Claude Haiku, Sonnet, and Opus. The cost check was re-run at 30 reps with OpenAI and Gemini arms.\n- **Arms:** No skill (baseline), Caveman (a prose-compression skill), and Ponytail.\n- **Metrics:** Code lines, cost, and latency come from the API. Tokens are also reported.\n- **Calls:** Single-shot completions at default temperature.\n\n## How \"less code\" is measured\n\n- `loc.js` counts lines inside fenced code blocks in each response.\n- `correctness.js` is a gate. It extracts the code blocks and runs checks. The email, debounce, and CSV checks execute the code with Python or Node. The React and FastAPI checks use only keyword or structural regex.\n\n## Controls\n\n- A no-skill baseline arm.\n- A second skill (Caveman) as a comparison arm.\n- Interleaved runs in the agentic benchmark.\n- Medians across repeated runs in the single-shot benchmark.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\n- The README says the single-shot gap \"counts prose, not just code, and overstates the win.\"\n- Single-shot cost does not reflect multi-turn sessions. Session cost \"can come out higher or lower.\"\n- The React and FastAPI checks are not executed, so they verify structure rather than correctness.\n- The tasks are everyday tasks, not production-grade specs.\n- An agentic A/B test found Ponytail \"can also raise tool calls and cost on completion-forced tasks.\"\n- Independent benchmarks are linked as corroboration only. The README notes their numbers \"may shift between runs.\"\n- Reproducing the single-shot benchmark requires Node.js ≥ 22.22.0 and an Anthropic API key. The `--env-file ../.env` flag is required.","durationMs":10680,"url":"https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail/tree/main/benchmarks"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6b7e5edd-d67c-4786-8828-ddefe976bfab","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"37f0b3b3-3e92-4a80-ad38-b5f000f92b0c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4uiAuKwiYPQm6ZjYGTp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2784,"cache_read_input_tokens":510635,"output_tokens":3428,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2208},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2784,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3428,"cache_read_input_tokens":510635,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2784,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2784},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":22426,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch4uiAcDWirzggBCNjAEw","requestRef":"d7ad930c-f040-4e57-8aec-e7b18ecac903","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"d2b63d65-16df-4dca-b1ec-63f04dc0e637","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:43.757Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"d2b63d65-16df-4dca-b1ec-63f04dc0e637","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch4uiAuKwiYPQm6ZjYGTp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I read the repo, its rules file and the benchmark README through a page summarizer. The per-task results file `results/2026-10-07-agentic.md` I couldn't see, so the numbers below are the README's own claims.\n\n[Ponytail](https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail) is an MIT-licensed instruction set that makes a coding agent write less code. Its rules file `AGENTS.md` (also a skill) is installed through per-harness adapters for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and others. The core is a ladder the agent climbs before writing anything. First it reads what the change touches and lists every place the change must reach. Then it asks:\n1. Does this need to exist?\n2. Is it already in the codebase?\n3. Does the standard library or platform cover it?\n4. Is there an installed dependency?\n5. Can it be one line?\n6. Only then: write the minimum that works.\n\n**Lessons for Nexus**\n- **The benchmark design is the part worth copying, more than the rules.**\n  - **Method:** it runs headless Claude Code on 39 tasks × 5 runs, with and without the skill, interleaved in one batch. It scores what the agent leaves behind and applies hidden correctness checks.\n  - **Claimed result:** code −53%, tokens −45%, cost −26%, time −41%. Hidden checks pass 97% against 96% without the skill.\n  - **Why it matters:** the correctness gate is what stops \"less code\" hiding a regression, and interleaving controls for drift. Our G1.1-4 results moved between runs for exactly that reason. MQ-8 should use both.\n- **It states its own limits.** The README says the single-shot gap \"overstates the win\" and that the skill \"can also raise tool calls and cost on completion-forced tasks\". Reporting adverse effects is a habit we should adopt in MQ-8.\n- **Ponytail's `AGENTS.md` is a ready-made test arm for MQ-8.** Nexus already loads `AGENTS.md` through `getProjectRules`. Running it on and off against our gateway models would check whether their numbers hold on local models. It's MIT licensed, but I haven't tested that it loads or behaves in Nexus.\n- **\"Reply ends with what was skipped or unchecked.\"** This matches our rule that completion reports coverage limits. A related idea is its `shortcut: <limit>, <when to upgrade>` code comments, collected by a command. A deterministic scan of a diff for new `shortcut:` markers could surface declared limits in the completion report. That is a small unit, and I haven't added it to the plan.\n- **A pressure rule paired with a protected list.** \"Never cut: validation at trust boundaries, data-loss error handling, security, accessibility.\" Our minimum-code principle should carry a similar protected list. Verification files are already protected deterministically (SI-2), but the principle text doesn't say so.\n- **Supply-chain care.** Ponytail itself warns to install only from official sources. Third-party rules pasted into `AGENTS.md` are an injection surface. Nexus gates project instructions behind workspace trust, which is the right default.\n\n**Caveats**\n- **Self-reported and narrow:** the agentic benchmark used one model (Opus 5.5), and I saw no intervals.\n- **Less code isn't better code:** it can hurt readability, and the repo itself notes cost can rise.\n- **Popularity isn't evidence:** the page shows about 191k stars, which doesn't measure outcomes.\n\nI haven't changed anything. If you want, I can add the Ponytail arm, interleaving and hidden checks to MQ-8 in the plans PR (#542), which is still on its required checks."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2784,"cache_read_input_tokens":510635,"output_tokens":3428,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2208},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2784,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3428,"cache_read_input_tokens":510635,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2784,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2784},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch4uiAcDWirzggBCNjAEw","requestRef":"d7ad930c-f040-4e57-8aec-e7b18ecac903","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa9ab320-f709-42db-b3c1-5e7ebd182dd9","timestamp":"2026-11-15T14:57:47.302Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
