{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","hash":"7c49ed9df0aee32ff1c9bf482ec756e7adab3c5395177a0e7bfb611c7a0b4f9c","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Goal \"resume\" acknowledged. I'm treating it as: stop waiting on the old gate and resume the v1.2.1 plan, following my earlier recommendation. That covers independent Phase 1 units in parallel, plus the steering-evaluation PR I proposed. I will not claim frontier quality at any point.\n\nFirst I'm launching three independent units in their own worktrees, following the process used for Wave 1. They are CX-5, VC-5 and WM-2."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012r1kBAWNtEk5M9qKBRz455","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"v1.2.1 CX-5 compaction invariant","prompt":"You are implementing unit CX-5 of the Nexus Code v1.2.1 plan, test-first, in its own git worktree. Repo: /home/mike/nexus-code (checkout is stale; work ONLY in your own worktree). Read AGENTS.md and docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md (row CX-5, and CX-3 for scope) on origin/main first.\n\nGOAL (CX-5): after every compaction, every gating requirement of the pinned objective ledger (`seedRequirements` in cli/src/core/objective.ts, `gatingRequirements`) is present in the request sent to the model, or the turn blocks with a named reason. This owns CERI scenario `compaction-03` (post-compaction check that every gating requirement is present); the scenario is currently pending in the CERI `semantic_integrity` domain (cli/src/ceri/, cli/test/ceri_semantic_integrity.test.ts): make it active.\nCoverage the acceptance requires: (1) a test that walks EVERY rung of the compaction ladder in `admitContext` (cli/src/agent.ts, ~line 4113: recentTurns lists [2,1,0] / [1,0] against detail budgets [6000,3000,1200,400,0] / [3000,1200,400,0]) and asserts the ledger survives each rung; (2) the `compact --llm` path (find it: grep compact in cli/src/slash/session_commands.ts and cli/src/session.ts); (3) an over-limit ledger (bigger than the context budget) is omitted whole and the turn BLOCKS with a named reason (use the existing outcome/reason vocabulary in cli/src/core/turn.ts), never truncated; (4) CERI scenario compaction-03 active and passing. First verify by reading code whether the ledger is actually carried inside `messages` or injected separately per request (the v1.2.0 audit found no requirement loss, so this may be a guard plus a blocking path; do not invent a bug).\n\nPROCESS (mandatory):\n- `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/cx5 -b v1/cx5-compaction-invariant origin/main`, then `ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/cx5/cli/node_modules` (pc2 has an installed node_modules).\n- Write failing tests first, then the minimal code. Run tests with a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<file>.test.ts`. Any test that builds an AutonomousAgent, RustCoreBridge or gateway must `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\"` and shut them down. Run the affected files on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH). `npm --prefix cli run build` (tsc) must be clean. Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git); compare against origin/main for any file you doubt before calling it a defect, and CI is the arbiter.\n- XERJ reference coding: the local XERJ node (`systemctl --user is-active xerj`, docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md) — query for how peers verify state after compaction (e.g. Gemini CLI chatCompressionService, Codex retain_across_windows, Cline) and record queries and what you took/rejected in the PR body \"Reference coding\" section.\n- Rules: chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`; never touch ~/.config/nexus/config.env or any credential file; never use the gateway or any inference (not needed); do not merge, tag, publish, or force-push. Commit with the trailer line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Push your branch and open a PR to main with `gh pr create` using the template sections of .github/pull_request_template.md; END the PR body with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`. CAPTURE the PR number from the `gh pr create` output (or `gh pr list --head <branch>`); do not guess it.\n- Keep the change minimal and touch only what you must. Preserve existing comments.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise): PR number and head SHA, files changed, what you found about where the ledger lives, test results with pass/fail TOTALS read from the output, Node 22 and 24 results, anything you could not verify."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018QLsN9Pw8c6D7u7gxMgXVL","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"v1.2.1 VC-5 verified re-check on resume","prompt":"You are implementing unit VC-5 of the Nexus Code v1.2.1 plan, test-first, in its own git worktree. Repo: /home/mike/nexus-code (checkout is stale; work ONLY in your own worktree). Read AGENTS.md and docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md (row VC-5) and docs/releases/v1.2.0.md \"Known limitations\" on origin/main first.\n\nGOAL (VC-5): (a) re-check `verified` on resume: a requirement marked verified must be demoted when the candidate identity differs on resume (workspace edited since verification); (b) persist the \"unbound-pass\" marker so a resumed task does not hit the \"no matching pre-change baseline\" refusal and completes as REGRESSION_FREE (v1.2.0 limitation: after a restart following an unbound-verifier pass the baseline is memory-only, so the resumed task is refused and only a fresh task recovers). Find the relevant code: grep in cli/src for `verified`, `requirementDone`, `REGRESSION_FREE`, `unbound`, `baseline`, `candidate identity` (cli/src/core/objective.ts, cli/src/core/verification*.ts, cli/src/agent.ts, cli/src/core/turn.ts, cli/src/core/receipts*). Acceptance: resume test: an edited workspace demotes `verified`; resume after an unbound pass completes as REGRESSION_FREE.\n\nPROCESS (mandatory):\n- `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc5 -b v1/vc5-resume-verified origin/main`, then `ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/vc5/cli/node_modules`.\n- Write failing tests first, then the minimal code. Run tests with a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<file>.test.ts`. Any test that builds an AutonomousAgent, RustCoreBridge or gateway must `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\"` and shut them down. Run affected files on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH). `npm --prefix cli run build` (tsc) must be clean. Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git); compare against origin/main for any file you doubt, CI is the arbiter.\n- Candidate identity must use the existing mechanism (find how the candidate identity / worktree digest is computed for receipts; do not invent a second one). Persisted markers need integrity consideration consistent with the existing durable-file helpers (cli/src/core/durable_file.ts).\n- XERJ reference coding: query the local XERJ node (`systemctl --user is-active xerj`, docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md) for how peers invalidate stale state on resume (e.g. opencode, Cline, Codex rollout/resume) and record queries and what you took/rejected in the PR body \"Reference coding\" section.\n- Rules: chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`; never touch ~/.config/nexus/config.env or credential files; no inference/gateway use; do not merge, tag, publish or force-push. Commit trailer: `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Push your branch, open a PR to main with `gh pr create` using .github/pull_request_template.md sections, END the body with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`. CAPTURE the PR number from the `gh pr create` output (or `gh pr list --head <branch>`); do not guess it.\n- Minimal change; preserve existing comments.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise): PR number and head SHA, files changed, design (what identity, where persisted), test results with pass/fail TOTALS read from output, Node 22 and 24 results, anything not verified."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014xZAaXAJw2MCpMtZMjWshJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"v1.2.1 WM-2 edit-failure feedback","prompt":"You are implementing unit WM-2 of the Nexus Code v1.2.1 plan, test-first, in its own git worktree. Repo: /home/mike/nexus-code (checkout is stale; work ONLY in your own worktree). Read AGENTS.md and docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md (row WM-2) on origin/main first.\n\nGOAL (WM-2): when an `edit_file` does not apply (the old text is not found, or matches ambiguously), the tool result returns the closest actual lines from the file so a weak model can correct itself (reference: Aider `editblock_coder.py` lines ~100-312: it finds the most similar chunk and reports it, with leading-whitespace and elision tolerance hints). Acceptance: tests with whitespace mismatches and elision mismatches. Find the edit tool: grep `edit_file` in cli/src/tools.ts and cli/src/file_diff.ts and cli/src/core/capability_definitions/. Constraints: the added feedback is bounded in size (cap lines and characters), is never produced for files the agent may not read (respect the existing read/permission/protected-path rules, e.g. do not echo content of a protected or secret-like file), is clearly labelled as file content (untrusted data, not instructions), and does not change successful-edit behaviour. Edits that fail must still fail (never \"fuzzy apply\"): feedback only.\n\nPROCESS (mandatory):\n- `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm2 -b v1/wm2-edit-failure-feedback origin/main`, then `ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm2/cli/node_modules`.\n- Failing tests first, then minimal code. Run tests with a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<file>.test.ts`. Tests that build an AutonomousAgent, RustCoreBridge or gateway must `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\"` and shut them down. Run affected files on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH). `npm --prefix cli run build` (tsc) must be clean. Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git): compare against origin/main for any file you doubt, CI is the arbiter. No new runtime dependency: implement similarity matching yourself (a bounded line-window comparison is enough); no third-party package.\n- XERJ reference coding: query the local XERJ node (`systemctl --user is-active xerj`, docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md) for Aider editblock, opencode and Cline edit-failure handling, and record queries and what you took/rejected in the PR body \"Reference coding\" section. Respect licence tags; copy no code verbatim.\n- Rules: chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`; never touch ~/.config/nexus/config.env or credential files; no inference/gateway use; do not merge, tag, publish or force-push. Commit trailer: `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Push your branch, open a PR to main with `gh pr create` using .github/pull_request_template.md sections, END the body with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`. CAPTURE the PR number from the `gh pr create` output (or `gh pr list --head <branch>`); do not guess it.\n- Minimal change; preserve existing comments.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise): PR number and head SHA, files changed, the bounds you chose and why, test results with pass/fail TOTALS read from output, Node 22 and 24 results, anything not verified."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
