{"parentUuid":"7a466f5c-c776-4f4b-8c9c-cf645d83caa8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"bc887f1a-ec4c-422e-91c3-baa444747751","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a7e5c607f9ed68b11</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_018QLsN9Pw8c6D7u7gxMgXVL</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68/tasks/a7e5c607f9ed68b11.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"v1.2.1 VC-5 verified re-check on resume\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>VC-5 is done: PR #547 is open against main, head SHA `f9d97e860897a1cd0a5d6c4088d7a29042944040`. Not merged. Checks run on the PR: 140 tests across 7 files, 140 pass, 0 fail, on Node 22.22.2 and on Node 24.21.0. `npm --prefix cli run build` (tsc) is clean.\n\n**Design**\n- **(a) Identity:** I reused the existing candidate identity, `computeCandidateIdentitySync(...).candidateId`, via `verifierState()`. It is already what `requirement.verifiedBy.inputs` records. On resume a `verified` requirement keeps that status only if its recorded `inputs` equal the current identity; otherwise, or if the identity can't be computed, it is demoted to `implemented`. The check is a new private `recheckOnResume()` in `cli/src/agent.ts`, called from `recoverFromTaskState` and the session-restore path. It goes through the existing `invalidateVerified`, which gets one optional `keep` predicate.\n- **(b) Persistence:** the unbound reason (`verifierUnbindableReason`) is now written into the existing durable `changeBaseline` record (two persist sites in `agent.ts`, plus the types in `cli/src/core/state.ts`). On resume it is read back into `recoveredUnboundVerifier`.\n- **How the resumed task completes:**\n  - The \"no matching pre-change baseline\" gate honours the marker only if the verifier command matches and `snapshotVerifierDefinitionWithReason` says the definition is still unbindable now. A forged marker therefore can't excuse a verifier that is bindable.\n  - The recovered baseline itself is still never trusted: `changeBaseline` stays null on resume.\n  - The resumed-work check that blocked REGRESSION_FREE (`!this.verificationBaselineInvalidated`) now lets an unbound pass through, so the task completes as REGRESSION_FREE and never VERIFIED.\n- **Integrity:** `durable_file.ts` only does atomic writes, so there is no integrity primitive to use. The marker is therefore only ever honoured in the weakening direction.\n\n**Files changed**\n- `cli/src/agent.ts`\n- `cli/src/core/objective.ts`\n- `cli/src/core/state.ts`\n- `cli/test/resume_verified_unbound.test.ts` (new, 3 tests)\n\n**Tests**\n- The new tests were red before the change (2 failed, and the forged-marker guard passed) and green after (3 of 3).\n- The three tests:\n  - An edited workspace demotes `verified`; an unchanged one keeps it.\n  - Resume after an unbound pass completes REGRESSION_FREE.\n  - A forged marker is still refused.\n- The seven files run on both Node versions are `resume_verified_unbound`, `unbound_verifier_completion`, `durable_continuity`, `objective_continuation`, `suspend_resume_acceptance`, `state` and `task_state_v2`.\n- The Node 22 totals come from the TAP summary and the Node 24 totals from its `ℹ` reporter lines.\n\n**Not verified**\n- I did not run the full `make test` or `make test-ci`; CI is the arbiter.\n- The write_file-based tests failed on this host until I built the Rust core with `cargo build --release` into the worktree's own target directory. The existing `unbound_verifier_completion` test failed the same way before the build, so that was the host, not this change.\n- XERJ reference coding was thin. The local node only has opencode, awesome-ai-system-prompts and serverless-workflow-spec indexed, so Cline and Codex resume could not be consulted. The PR's \"Reference coding\" section records the two queries and that gap. I took only opencode's idea of comparing resumed state against a recorded snapshot identity.\n- A resumed turn with no new edits still ends as `UNVERIFIED` / `VERIFICATION_NOT_RUN`. That is existing behaviour and I left it alone; my resume test makes an edit in the second turn.\n- No independent review has been done.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>148724</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>67</tool_uses><duration_ms>1175837</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"f344e5a4-7751-4cfe-b4b1-7351be1e46b2","timestamp":"2026-11-15T17:12:20.167Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task","runId":"0mwi2ohcy-c10027ef"},"promptSource":"system","turnOrigin":"task_notification","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":95,"turnIndex":541},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
