{"parentUuid":"de5567ef-c19e-4cd9-8d5f-04902f66a15f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch58u69QNAdwN5KDc9mg4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"#546 passed as well, but I'm holding the merges while the main CI run finishes; I'm starting a small client-side fix agent now, since it doesn't touch the gateway.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3031,"cache_read_input_tokens":687241,"output_tokens":3207,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1074},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3031,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3207,"cache_read_input_tokens":687241,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3031,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3031},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch58u5r3WBhcZ6ddXpP3h","requestRef":"9cfd9cbd-733e-44fd-8577-77bb1e118dda","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"52055926-cdca-42c1-9a20-c96410e8437b","timestamp":"2026-11-15T17:50:28.934Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"52055926-cdca-42c1-9a20-c96410e8437b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch58u69QNAdwN5KDc9mg4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B7NTtiusnA6mj92P6psKNA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"v1.2.1 WM-4 function-call-text recovery","prompt":"You are implementing a new unit (WM-4) for Nexus Code v1.2.1, test-first, in its own git worktree. Repo: /home/mike/nexus-code (checkout is stale; work ONLY in your own worktree). Read AGENTS.md first (on origin/main), and docs/v1.2.1/PLAN.md rows WM-1 and WM-2 for context.\n\nEVIDENCE (observed 2026-11-15 on the shared gateway, alias `engineering/lead-reasoning`): for the plain prompt \"Call the read_file tool exactly once with arguments that satisfy its supplied JSON Schema. Do not add prose.\" the model returned NO native tool_calls (calls=0) and its text content was literally `read_file(path=\"README.md\")` (python-style function-call text), 10 of 10 times. Four hours earlier the same prompt returned a native tool call. In the live G1.1-4 matrix this made 45 of 50 read_file attempts \"invalid\" for that alias (155/200 overall), while the other alias, which writes the call as text in a form the client recognises, recovers via the client's feedback retry (feedbackRetries 45) and ends 200/200. So the client's call-as-text recognition does not cover this form, and the safe feedback path never engages.\n\nGOAL: recognise function-call-style text as a \"call written as text\" so the EXISTING safe policy applies. Policy today (do not change it): a reply that was the call as text is never executed; the client tells the model and asks again, then forces tool_choice=required (see the client message \"endpoint answered a tool request with the call as text; telling it so and asking again\", `toolCallSource` \"feedback\"/\"required\" in cli/src/client.ts, and cli/src/core/content_tool_call.ts). Find where the client decides a content reply \"is a call as text\" (grep content_tool_call, toolCallSource, feedback in cli/src/client.ts, cli/src/core/content_tool_call.ts, cli/src/protocols/) and extend the recognition to: (1) a reply whose whole content (after trimming, optionally inside one markdown code fence) is exactly ONE call `name(arg=\"value\", arg2=123, flag=true, items=[...], obj={...})` to a tool that was OFFERED in this request; (2) the same wrapped in a list `[name(...)]` (the \"pythonic\" tool-call format used by some Llama-family chat templates; multiple comma-separated calls in the list are also a call-as-text). Conservative by design: only whole-content matches, only names of offered tools, balanced parentheses/quotes, literal arguments only (strings in single or double quotes with escapes, numbers, true/false/null/True/False/None, lists, dicts); prose that merely MENTIONS a call (a sentence, a call inside a longer explanation, a call in a code block with surrounding text, an unknown function name) must NOT be recognised. Never execute anything: recognition only triggers the existing feedback/forced retry path. If the existing path also parses arguments out of recognised text calls (check), reuse it for this form; do not add a second execution route. Do NOT touch eval/ or the G1.1-4 matrix classification (it must keep counting only native calls after retries).\n\nTESTS (failing first): a table of positive forms (`read_file(path=\"README.md\")`, with fence, with `[...]`, single-quoted strings, nested list/dict args, a True/None literal) and negatives (prose mentioning the call, unknown tool, unbalanced, call with a variable argument like path=README, trailing explanation), plus a client-level test with the mock gateway fixture (cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts startGateway) that answers the first request with `read_file(path=\"README.md\")` as content and the second with a native call, asserting the feedback retry happens and the result is native. No real gateway or inference; tests that start gateways/agents must `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\"` and close them.\n\nPROCESS (mandatory): `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm4 -b v1/wm4-function-call-text origin/main && ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/wm4/cli/node_modules`. Run tests with a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<file>.test.ts`; run the affected files AND the existing tool-call/client files (grep for content_tool_call, tool_grammar, request_shape, harness tests) on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH) and compare any failure against origin/main before calling it a defect (host noise exists: unbuilt cli/dist/Rust core; CI is the arbiter). `npm --prefix cli run build` must be clean. XERJ reference coding (docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md; node must be active): query how peers recover tool calls from text (goose toolshim, opencode tool-input-repair, llama.cpp pythonic tool parsers if indexed) and record queries and what you took/rejected in the PR \"Reference coding\" section; copy no code. Rules: chain with &&; never `git stash`; never touch ~/.config/nexus/config.env or credential files; no gateway or inference; do not merge, tag, publish or force-push. Commit trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Push and open a PR to main with `gh pr create` using .github/pull_request_template.md sections, END the body with `🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)`. CAPTURE the PR number from the `gh pr create` output or `gh pr list --head <branch>`; never guess it.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise): PR number and head SHA, where recognition lives, the exact accepted/rejected forms, test results with pass/fail TOTALS read from the output on Node 22 and 24, anything not verified."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_detail