{"parentUuid":"66a9064d-db5e-40d5-b0fe-978a6f176a43","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch5DKvA3bZTrvyPAQHExu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That monitor (`b0chc2eij`) ended because both of its PRs merged and the `main` run it watched concluded. It was a normal completion, not a failure. The remaining watch is `bf7p8qkon`, on #547's run.\n\nNo new action is needed. I'm waiting on #547's last job, and then I'll verify and merge it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_read_input_tokens":736939,"output_tokens":111,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":373,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":111,"cache_read_input_tokens":736939,"cache_creation_input_tokens":373,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":373},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch5DKuhXEEk1K1ZntiadZ","requestRef":"33258a57-cd0b-476e-bd3e-dbf86b06a14f","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"bc9d39a9-eefd-4c23-89be-401837645f33","timestamp":"2026-11-15T18:48:23.059Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-15T18:48:41.983Z","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","content":"<pasted_content id=\"2765\">\n# Task: harden command execution and the sandbox in nexus-code\nYou are working in the nexus-code repository (TypeScript CLI in `cli/`, Rust core in `core/`). A static review of `main` at 03bf579 found the defects below. Fix them as separate, reviewable slices. Each slice gets its own branch and PR.\n## Ground rules (from AGENTS.md; follow them exactly)\n- Read `AGENTS.md`, `docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md` and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §3 before you start.\n- One worktree and branch per slice, under `~/nexus-wt/`, never `~/nexus-code` itself. Claim each slice or hot file under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` before you edit. Check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Isolate `HOME` for every test or probe: `HOME=$(mktemp -d)`. Never touch the real `~/.config/nexus`.\n- Use a separate `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` for each worktree.\n- Stop processes by their own pid, never with `pkill -f`.\n- Reference coding: these slices touch sandbox, process and git handling, so query XERJ as `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` describes. Compare with how mature agents (Codex CLI, Claude Code, OpenHands, Aider) handle environment scrubbing, bubblewrap profiles and git hooks. Record what you took, borrowed or rejected in each PR's \"Reference coding\" section. If XERJ is unavailable, write \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" and use `git grep`.\n- Do not push, tag, merge or publish unless the owner tells you to. Open PRs only when asked; otherwise leave the branches ready and report.\n- Done for each slice means: a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after; the affected test files pass on Node 22 and Node 24; `npm --prefix cli run build` is clean; `cargo test` and `cargo clippy` are clean for Rust changes. Preserve existing comments and unrelated code.\n- Report results as measured. If something can't be reproduced or verified, say so rather than claiming a fix.\n## Slice 1: keep credentials out of agent commands (High)\n**Problem.** `cli/src/config.ts` loads `~/.config/nexus/config.env` into `process.env` with override. That includes provider keys such as `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`, plus the gateway token setting. `cli/src/tools.ts` (around line 791) starts `harness-core` with no `env`, so it inherits all of it. `core/src/runner.rs` (around line 580) doesn't clear the environment. Only the read-only bwrap path uses `--clearenv`. So `run_command \"env\"` prints the keys, with or without a sandbox.\n**Fix.**\n1. Add one function that builds the command environment, for example `buildCommandEnvironment()` in a new `cli/src/command_env.ts`. Model it on the existing `buildLspEnvironment` and `buildMcpEnvironment`, and reuse their rules if they fit. It must remove:\n   - every key that came from `config.env` (`userConfigFile` in `config.ts`) or from the credential authority in `client.ts`;\n   - keys matching credential patterns: `*_API_KEY`, `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET`, `*_PASSWORD`, `AWS_*`, `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, `NEXUS_GATEWAY_TOKEN*`, `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `NPM_TOKEN`, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN`;\n   - anything that points at a credential file.\n   It must keep `PATH`, `HOME`, `LANG`/`LC_*`, `TERM`, `USER`, `SHELL`, `TMPDIR` and ordinary toolchain variables. Add an operator-only passthrough, `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (comma-separated names). Read it from the operator environment or `config.env` only, never from a project `.env`.\n2. Start `harness-core` with that environment. Make the `executeViaSpawnSync` fallback use the same environment so the two paths can't drift.\n3. In `runner.rs`, as a second layer: clear the environment when the RPC request carries an explicit env, or strip the same name patterns. Keep this consistent with the TypeScript rules and comment why it exists.\n4. Write-mode bwrap: add `--clearenv` and set back only the scrubbed environment with `--setenv`.\n**Tests.** Put a sentinel key (for example `REDACTED in a temp `HOME`'s `config.env`. Run `run_command \"env\"` through the real tool path in three modes: unsandboxed, `--sandbox` with bwrap, and the spawnSync fallback. Assert the sentinel never appears in the output. Also assert that a passthrough variable does appear.\n## Slice 2: make write-mode `--sandbox` actually confine (High)\n**Problem.** In `cli/src/sandbox.ts`, the non-read-only bwrap branch (around lines 312–328) uses `--ro-bind / /`, so the whole host is readable, including `~/.ssh`, `~/.config/nexus`, `~/.aws` and `~/.gnupg`. The network is cut only when `network === false`, and `tools.ts:1594` passes `undefined` outside read-only mode. Egress approval covers only the fetch tool; shell `curl`/`wget` are never checked.\n**Fix.**\n1. Mask `$HOME` in write mode the same way the read-only path does (`--tmpfs $HOME`). Then bind back read-only only the toolchain directories that real builds need. Detect them from `PATH` entries under `$HOME` and from a fixed list (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm` caches, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`). Never bind `~/.ssh`, `~/.gnupg`, `~/.aws`, `~/.config/nexus`, `~/.config/gh`, `~/.netrc`, `~/.docker/config.json`, `~/.kube`, or browser profiles. Let the operator extend the allowlist (operator config only), and show the effective mounts in `nexus doctor`.\n2. Turn the network off by default under `--sandbox` (`--unshare-net`). Add an explicit opt-in, such as `--sandbox-network` and/or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` (operator-only). In `review` mode, a command that needs the network asks for approval and the prompt says so. Under `--yes` the opt-in must still be explicit; don't turn the network on silently.\n3. Add `--die-with-parent` and `--new-session` to both bwrap branches. `ARCHITECTURE.md` already claims `--die-with-parent`; make that true. `--new-session` blocks TIOCSTI terminal injection. Check that PTY-mode commands still work with `--new-session`. If they don't, explain why and limit the change accordingly.\n4. Keep the workspace bind writable, but see Slice 4 for `.git`.\n**Tests.** With bwrap available (CI installs it; skip clearly if it's missing):\n- A sentinel file at `$HOME/.ssh/id_test` can't be read from a sandboxed command.\n- `getent hosts example.com` or a connect to `1.1.1.1:53` fails by default and works with the opt-in.\n- `cargo --version` and `node --version` still work when those toolchains live under `$HOME`.\n- A backgrounded child is gone after the parent is killed.\nRun `scripts/terminal-acceptance` and the evaluator tests as well: they use the sandbox and are the most likely to break.\n## Slice 3: make ARCHITECTURE.md match the code (Medium)\nUpdate `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` §3, items 3 and 6:\n- State that egress approval covers network tools, not shell commands. The shell is confined only by the sandbox's network setting.\n- Describe exactly what write-mode `--sandbox` masks and binds, what the environment contains, and the network default with its opt-in.\n- Fix the `--die-with-parent` claim to match the code after Slice 2.\nUpdate `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `README.md` (the \"Defense-in-Depth Sandboxing\" and \"auto mode\" bullets) and `CHANGELOG.md` to match. Run `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs` and `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`.\n## Slice 4: guard the legacy worktree merge against planted git config and hooks (Medium; reproduce first)\n**Suspected problem.** `WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree` in `cli/src/worktree.ts` (around lines 410–425) runs plain `git status`, `git add -A` and `git commit` inside the agent-writable worktree. It uses no `trustedGitArgs`, no `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, no `core.fsmonitor=false`, and no `git_hook_guard`. It's called from `agent.ts:1922`. The newer paths (`cli/src/worktrunk/git.ts`, `cli/src/git.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_git.ts`) already guard against this.\n**Step 1: reproduce.** In a temp `HOME` and temp repository: create a linked worktree the way Nexus does. As the agent could with `run_command` in `auto` mode, replace the worktree's `.git` file with a directory containing a `config` with `core.fsmonitor` or `core.hooksPath` and a `pre-commit` hook that touches a sentinel file outside the worktree. Then trigger `mergeWorktree`. Record whether the sentinel appears. If it doesn't, write down why (which existing check stops it), add that as a regression test anyway, and stop this slice.\n**Step 2: fix if it reproduces.**\n- Run `verifyCheckout`/`trustedGitArgs` before any git call in `mergeWorktree`, and pin `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`.\n- Pass the same hardening `-c` flags and environment that `worktrunk/git.ts` uses, including `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, `core.fsmonitor=false`, the Git 2.54+ hook switches from `git_hook_guard.ts`, and `NON_INTERACTIVE`.\n- Better: send this path through the `worktrunk` merge implementation and delete the duplicate. If that's too big, say so in the PR and leave a tracked follow-up.\n- Grep for every other plain `execFileSync(\"git\", ...)` in `cli/src` that runs inside agent-writable directories without the guard. List each one in the PR, and fix it or explain why it's safe.\n## Slice 5: harden the Docker sandbox (Low)\nIn `cli/src/sandbox.ts`, Docker branch (around lines 345–386):\n- Replace `alpine:latest` with a digest-pinned image. Make it operator-configurable, and keep the pin in one place alongside the other pins.\n- Add `--user $(id -u):$(id -g)`, `--cap-drop ALL`, `--security-opt no-new-privileges`, `--network none` by default (same opt-in as Slice 2), `--pids-limit`, and `--read-only` with a writable tmpfs `/tmp`.\n- Pass the scrubbed environment from Slice 1 explicitly, with `--env` per variable and nothing inherited.\n- Remove the dead `mountMode` code; `readOnlyHost` returns before it's reached.\n- Update `dockerSandboxProbe` (around line 200) so it still passes.\n## Slice 6: protected-path matching uses workspace-relative paths (Low)\n`SecurityManager.isPathProtected` in `cli/src/security.ts` (around lines 457–530) checks every segment of the absolute and resolved paths. A workspace under, say, `~/.env-tools/proj` or `~/id_rsa-backup/proj` gets every file blocked. Match patterns against the path relative to the workspace root (and relative to the resolved root for the symlink check). Keep these rules: a symlink inside the workspace that resolves to a protected target is still refused, and a symlink that resolves outside the workspace is still refused. Add tests for both false-positive layouts and for the cases that must stay blocked (`.git/hooks/x`, `sub/.env.local`, a symlink to `.git`).\n## Slice 7: remove `allowExternalSymlinks` (Low)\n`allowExternalSymlinks` is parsed in `security.ts` but never read. Remove it from the policy type and parser, unless a doc promises it does something; in that case, ask the owner before implementing it. A workspace-sourced policy may only tighten rules, never loosen them. Keep it that way.\n## Order and report\nDo the slices in order 1 → 2 → 3, then 4–7 in any order. Slices 1 and 2 both touch `sandbox.ts` and `tools.ts`, so land 1 before starting 2, and don't run them in parallel.\nWhen you finish, give the owner one short report per slice:\n- branch name and head SHA;\n- what changed;\n- the tests that were added, and their pass/fail on Node 22 and 24;\n- for Slice 4, the reproduction result;\n- anything you couldn't verify;\n- any behaviour change users will notice (network off by default, masked `HOME`), with a proposed `CHANGELOG.md` entry.\n</pasted_content id=\"2765\">"}
