in. Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Report as measured.\n\nSLICE 6: protected-path matching uses workspace-relative paths (Low).\n`SecurityManager.isPathProtected` in `cli/src/security.ts` (around lines 457-530) checks every segment of the absolute and resolved paths. A workspace under, say, `~/.env-tools/proj` or `~/id_rsa-backup/proj` gets every file blocked. Match patterns against the path relative to the workspace root (and relative to the resolved root for the symlink check). Keep these rules: a symlink inside the workspace that resolves to a protected target is still refused, and a symlink that resolves outside the workspace is still refused. Add tests for both false-positive layouts (workspace under `.env-tools/` and under `id_rsa-backup/`) and for the cases that must stay blocked (`.git/hooks/x`, `sub/.env.local`, a symlink to `.git`).\n\nSLICE 7: remove `allowExternalSymlinks` (Low).\n`allowExternalSymlinks` is parsed in `security.ts` but never read. Remove it from the policy type and parser, UNLESS a doc promises it does something (grep docs/, README.md, CHANGELOG.md, the manual): in that case do not implement or remove it, and report that the owner must decide. A workspace-sourced policy may only tighten rules, never loosen them; keep it that way (add a test if none covers it).\n\nREPORT BACK (concise, one block per slice): branch name and head SHA; what changed; whether the defect reproduced on current main (the exact observation); tests added with before/after result and pass/fail TOTALS on Node 22 and 24; anything you could not verify; any user-visible behaviour change with a proposed CHANGELOG.md entry.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68/tasks/a09dc5ad3da83efc4.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"834ec99a-679c-4bc9-8c77-18c8d066b942","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","hash":"c5115155d32c11ec8fc22bbf8030e047b03ea3b609a12dc69e61e8d2aa2e58be","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'm treating this as your task and starting on it. Two points about how I'll run it:\n- **Following the paste's own ground rules:** branches stay local and ready, with no pushes and no PRs until you ask.\n- **Sequencing:** Slices 1 and 2 both touch `sandbox.ts` and `tools.ts`, so I won't run them in parallel. I'll start the three independent ones now: Slice 1, Slice 4 (worktree merge, reproduce first) and Slices 6 and 7 (`security.ts`, together because they share a file). Slices 2, 3 and 5 follow Slice 1 as ordered."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018BEdaMuECYMiSKmy2braGY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hardening slice 1: scrub command environment","prompt":"You are implementing Slice 1 of a security-hardening task in the nexus-code repository (TypeScript CLI in `cli/`, Rust core in `core/`), test-first, in your own git worktree. A static review of main found the defect below; VERIFY it on current origin/main before fixing (reproduce it), and report honestly if it does not reproduce.\n\nGROUND RULES (from AGENTS.md; read it, docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3 first):\n- Worktree: `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard1 -b v1/hardening-s1-command-env origin/main && ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard1/cli/node_modules`. Never edit ~/nexus-code itself. Claim the slice/hot files under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` exactly as docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md describes (atomic mkdir + owner.json + a line in nexus-claims.log) before editing; when you finish, release the claim the way earlier ones were (\"released-<timestamp>\" naming, see `ls` of that directory). Check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Isolate HOME for every test or probe: `HOME=$(mktemp -d)`. Never touch the real ~/.config/nexus. Use `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/claude-1000/cargo-hard1` for Rust. Stop processes by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`.\n- Do NOT push, tag, merge, publish or open a PR. Commit locally on your branch and leave it ready (commit trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`).\n- Reference coding: query XERJ per docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md (node must be active: `systemctl --user is-active xerj`; first query can take over a minute) for how mature agents (Codex CLI, Claude Code, OpenHands, Aider, opencode, goose) scrub environments and handle bubblewrap `--clearenv`. Write what you took/borrowed/rejected into a file `docs/research/hardening-s1-reference-coding.md`-style note in your final report (and in the commit message body); if XERJ is unavailable say \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" and use git grep.\n- Done means: a regression test that FAILS before the fix and passes after; affected test files pass on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH); `npm --prefix cli run build` clean; `cargo test` and `cargo clippy` clean for Rust changes (run from `core/` with the isolated CARGO_TARGET_DIR). Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git): compare against origin/main before calling anything a defect. Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Report results as measured; say what you could not verify.\n\nSLICE 1: keep credentials out of agent commands (High).\nProblem: `cli/src/config.ts` loads `~/.config/nexus/config.env` into `process.env` with override. That includes provider keys such as `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`, plus the gateway token setting. `cli/src/tools.ts` (around line 791) starts `harness-core` with no `env`, so it inherits all of it. `core/src/runner.rs` (around line 580) does not clear the environment. Only the read-only bwrap path uses `--clearenv`. So `run_command \"env\"` prints the keys, with or without a sandbox.\nFix:\n1. One function that builds the command environment, e.g. `buildCommandEnvironment()` in a new `cli/src/command_env.ts`, modelled on the existing `buildLspEnvironment` and `buildMcpEnvironment` (reuse their rules if they fit; find them with grep). It must remove: every key that came from `config.env` (`userConfigFile` in config.ts) or from the credential authority in `client.ts`; keys matching `*_API_KEY`, `*_TOKEN`, `*_SECRET`, `*_PASSWORD`, `AWS_*`, `GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS`, `NEXUS_GATEWAY_TOKEN*`, `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `NPM_TOKEN`, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN`; and anything that points at a credential file. It must keep PATH, HOME, LANG/LC_*, TERM, USER, SHELL, TMPDIR and ordinary toolchain variables. Add an operator-only passthrough `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (comma-separated names), read ONLY from the operator environment or config.env, never from a project `.env`.\n2. Start `harness-core` with that environment. Make the `executeViaSpawnSync` fallback use the same environment so the two paths cannot drift.\n3. In `runner.rs`, as a second layer: clear the environment when the RPC request carries an explicit env, or strip the same name patterns. Keep it consistent with the TypeScript rules and comment why it exists.\n4. Write-mode bwrap: add `--clearenv` and set back only the scrubbed environment with `--setenv`. (Slice 2 will later rework the rest of the write-mode branch; keep your change to the environment only, so the two merge cleanly.)\nTests: put a sentinel key (e.g. `REDACTED in a temp HOME's config.env. Run `run_command \"env\"` through the real tool path in three modes: unsandboxed, `--sandbox` with bwrap (skip clearly if bwrap is not installed: `which bwrap`), and the spawnSync fallback. Assert the sentinel never appears in the output. Also assert a passthrough variable DOES appear. Any test that starts agents/bridges/gateways must `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\"` and shut them down.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise, one block): branch name and head SHA; what changed (files); whether the defect reproduced on current main (the exact observation); tests added and their before/after result and pass/fail TOTALS on Node 22 and 24; cargo test/clippy results; anything you could not verify; any user-visible behaviour change with a proposed CHANGELOG.md entry."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013Z5zGtuDJn1Qb4PyFzapv2","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hardening slice 4: legacy worktree merge git guard","prompt":"You are working on Slice 4 of a security-hardening task in the nexus-code repository (TypeScript CLI in `cli/`, Rust core in `core/`), in your own git worktree. This slice is REPRODUCE FIRST: a static review suspects a defect that may not be real, and an honest \"does not reproduce, here is the check that stops it\" is a valid outcome.\n\nGROUND RULES (from AGENTS.md; read it, docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3 first):\n- Worktree: `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard4 -b v1/hardening-s4-worktree-merge origin/main && ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard4/cli/node_modules`. Never edit ~/nexus-code itself. Claim the slice/hot files (cli/src/worktree.ts) under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` exactly as docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md describes (atomic mkdir + owner.json + a nexus-claims.log line) before editing; release the claim when done (\"released-<timestamp>\" naming, see `ls` of that directory). Check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Isolate HOME for every test or probe: `HOME=$(mktemp -d)`. Never touch the real ~/.config/nexus. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/claude-1000/cargo-hard4` if you build Rust. Stop processes by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`.\n- Do NOT push, tag, merge, publish or open a PR. Commit locally and leave the branch ready (trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`).\n- Reference coding: query XERJ per docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md (`systemctl --user is-active xerj`; first query can take over a minute) for how mature agents (Codex CLI, Claude Code, OpenHands, Aider) guard git against planted config/hooks in agent-writable worktrees; record what you took/borrowed/rejected in your final report and the commit message; if unavailable write \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" and use git grep.\n- Done means: a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after (or, if it does not reproduce, a regression test documenting the guard that stops it); affected test files pass on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH); `npm --prefix cli run build` clean. Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git): compare against origin/main. Preserve existing comments. Report as measured.\n\nSLICE 4: guard the legacy worktree merge against planted git config and hooks (Medium; reproduce first).\nSuspected problem: `WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree` in `cli/src/worktree.ts` (around lines 410-425) runs plain `git status`, `git add -A` and `git commit` inside the agent-writable worktree. It uses no `trustedGitArgs`, no `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, no `core.fsmonitor=false`, and no `git_hook_guard`. It is called from `agent.ts:1922`. The newer paths (`cli/src/worktrunk/git.ts`, `cli/src/git.ts`, `cli/src/core/operation_git.ts`) already guard against this.\nStep 1, reproduce: in a temp HOME and temp repository, create a linked worktree the way Nexus does. As the agent could with `run_command` in `auto` mode, replace the worktree's `.git` file with a directory containing a `config` with `core.fsmonitor` or `core.hooksPath` and a `pre-commit` hook that touches a sentinel file OUTSIDE the worktree. Then trigger `mergeWorktree`. Record whether the sentinel appears. If it does not, write down why (which existing check stops it), add that as a regression test anyway, and STOP this slice (report the result).\nStep 2, fix if it reproduces: run `verifyCheckout`/`trustedGitArgs` before any git call in `mergeWorktree`, and pin `--git-dir`/`--work-tree`; pass the same hardening `-c` flags and environment that `worktrunk/git.ts` uses, including `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, `core.fsmonitor=false`, the Git 2.54+ hook switches from `git_hook_guard.ts`, and `NON_INTERACTIVE`. Better, if feasible: send this path through the `worktrunk` merge implementation and delete the duplicate; if that is too big, say so and leave a tracked follow-up note in your report. Grep for every other plain `execFileSync(\"git\", ...)` / `spawnSync(\"git\"...)` in `cli/src` that runs inside agent-writable directories without the guard; list each in your report, and fix it or explain why it is safe.\n\nREPORT BACK (concise, one block): branch name and head SHA; the reproduction result (the exact observation, sentinel present or absent and why); what changed; tests added with before/after result and pass/fail TOTALS on Node 22 and 24; the list of other unguarded git call sites and your disposition for each; anything you could not verify; any user-visible behaviour change."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0113sYvtWFTnggb69Lp8gRn5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hardening slices 6 and 7: security.ts path matching and policy","prompt":"You are implementing Slices 6 and 7 of a security-hardening task in the nexus-code repository (TypeScript CLI in `cli/`), test-first, in your own git worktrees. Both slices touch `cli/src/security.ts`, so do 6 first (branch 1), then 7 (branch 2, based on branch 1 or origin/main as you judge cleanest; say which). VERIFY each defect on current origin/main before fixing and report honestly if one does not reproduce.\n\nGROUND RULES (from AGENTS.md; read it, docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3 first):\n- Worktrees: `cd /home/mike/nexus-code && git fetch origin && git worktree add /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard6 -b v1/hardening-s6-protected-paths origin/main && ln -s /home/mike/nexus-wt/pc2/cli/node_modules /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard6/cli/node_modules` (and similarly `hard7` / `v1/hardening-s7-external-symlinks`). Never edit ~/nexus-code itself. Claim the slice/hot file (cli/src/security.ts) under `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` exactly as docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md describes (atomic mkdir + owner.json + nexus-claims.log line) before editing; release it when done (\"released-<timestamp>\" naming, see `ls` of that directory). Check `git log` for foreign commits before each commit.\n- Isolate HOME for every test or probe: `HOME=$(mktemp -d)`. Never touch the real ~/.config/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Chain shell commands with &&; never `git stash`.\n- Do NOT push, tag, merge, publish or open a PR. Commit locally and leave the branches ready (trailer `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`).\n- Reference coding: query XERJ per docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md (`systemctl --user is-active xerj`; first query can take over a minute) for how mature agents (Codex CLI, Claude Code, OpenHands, Aider, opencode) match protected paths relative to the workspace and treat symlinks; record what you took/borrowed/rejected in your final report and commit messages; if unavailable write \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" and use git grep.\n- Done means: a regression test that FAILS before the fix and passes after; affected test files pass on Node 22 AND 24 (`~/.config/nvm/versions/node/v22.22.2/bin` and `.../v24.21.0/bin` first on PATH); `npm --prefix cli run build` clean. Some tests fail on this host for host reasons (unbuilt cli/dist, stray /tmp/.git): compare against origin/main. Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Report as measured.\n\nSLICE 6: protected-path matching uses workspace-relative paths (Low).\n`SecurityManager.isPathProtected` in `cli/src/security.ts` (around lines 457-530) checks every segment of the absolute and resolved paths. A workspace under, say, `~/.env-tools/proj` or `~/id_rsa-backup/proj` gets every file blocked. Match patterns against the path relative to the workspace root (and relative to the resolved root for the symlink check). Keep these rules: a symlink inside the workspace that resolves to a protected target is still refused, and a symlink that resolves outside the workspace is still refused. Add tests for both false-positive layouts (workspace under `.env-tools/` and under `id_rsa-backup/`) and for the cases that must stay blocked (`.git/hooks/x`, `sub/.env.local`, a symlink to `.git`).\n\nSLICE 7: remove `allowExternalSymlinks` (Low).\n`allowExternalSymlinks` is parsed in `security.ts` but never read. Remove it from the policy type and parser, UNLESS a doc promises it does something (grep docs/, README.md, CHANGELOG.md, the manual): in that case do not implement or remove it, and report that the owner must decide. A workspace-sourced policy may only tighten rules, never loosen them; keep it that way (add a test if none covers it).\n\nREPORT BACK (concise, one block per slice): branch name and head SHA; what changed; whether the defect reproduced on current main (the exact observation); tests added with before/after result and pass/fail TOTALS on Node 22 and 24; anything you could not verify; any user-visible behaviour change with a proposed CHANGELOG.md entry."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
