{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-15T19:03:52.716Z","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>b3z2qk3lk</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01NUJ7jCZq76qaqP9YXMpcN4</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68/tasks/b3z2qk3lk.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Background command \"cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/hard4/cli &amp;&amp; T=(test/worktree_merge_planted_git.test.ts test/candidate.test.ts test/harness.test.ts test/worktrunk_binding.test.ts test/worktrunk_review.test.ts test/recover_worktree_conversation.test.ts); for v in v22.22.2 v24.21.0; do echo \"-- $v\"; PATH=/home/mike/.config/nvm/versions/node/$v/bin:$PATH node scripts/run_tests.mjs $T 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -E \"^✖|^ℹ (tests|pass|fail)\"; done; rtk git status --short\" completed (exit code 0)</summary>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-15T19:03:59.590Z","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a09dc5ad3da83efc4</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_0113sYvtWFTnggb69Lp8gRn5</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68/tasks/a09dc5ad3da83efc4.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Hardening slices 6 and 7: security.ts path matching and policy\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Both slices are committed locally and not pushed, and the claim on `cli/src/security.ts` is released. Slice 6 reproduced and is fixed. Slice 7 needed no owner decision.\n\nBranch 2 is stacked on branch 1, because both edit `cli/src/security.ts`. I ran the tests and the build in the worktree, not the whole-repo suite.\n\n## Slice 6: protected paths relative to the workspace\n- **Branch and head:** `v1/hardening-s6-protected-paths` at `7de595ed`, based on origin/main `897fd530`.\n- **Reproduced on main:** yes. Workspaces under `.env-tools/`, `id_rsa-backup/` and `.git/` each had `main.ts` and `sub/a.ts` reported as protected.\n- **What changed:**\n  - `isPathProtected` now matches patterns against the path relative to the workspace root. The symlink check matches against the resolved root, and a path that resolves outside the root is matched on its absolute path.\n  - That last rule is how symlinks to protected targets outside the workspace stay refused, so such a symlink is refused only if its target path contains a protected name. This is stricter than the old behaviour, which could never allow it.\n  - Absolute policy patterns still match the absolute path.\n  - Both the real and lexical workspace roots are tried, in case the workspace sits behind a symlink such as `/tmp`.\n- **Tests added** (`cli/test/security.test.ts`, 7 tests):\n  - Workspace under `.env-tools/`, `id_rsa-backup/` and `.git/` is not blocked wholesale, while `.git/hooks/x`, `sub/.env.local` and `id_rsa` inside it stay blocked.\n  - Protected names in an ordinary workspace stay blocked.\n  - A symlink to `.git` is refused.\n  - A symlink resolving outside the workspace to a protected target is refused.\n  - A workspace policy can only add protected paths.\n- **Before and after:** before the fix 3 of the new tests failed, the three layouts. After, they all pass.\n- **Totals:** `security.test.ts` plus `specialist_*.test.ts` gives 222 tests, 221 pass and 1 fail, on both Node 22.22.2 and 24.21.0.\n- **The one failure:** `RustCoreBridge refuses read tools on .env.local...`. It fails identically before the fix and fails at `security.test.ts:94`, the plain `read_file` of `normal.txt`, the same place as before the fix. It looks like the unbuilt Rust core, but I did not rebuild it to confirm.\n- **Build:** `npm --prefix cli run build` is clean.\n\n## Slice 7: remove `allowExternalSymlinks`\n- **Branch and head:** `v1/hardening-s7-external-symlinks` at `4a09556f`, based on branch 1.\n- **Reproduced:** it is parsed and set to false but never read, which is the defect. The only mentions outside `security.ts` are an audit note under `docs/v1.0.0/evidence/` and a test fixture name. README, the manual and CHANGELOG do not promise it, and the manual's symlink confinement text refers to the Rust core, so I removed it.\n- **What changed:** the policy type field and both parser sites are gone from `security.ts` (3 deletions). The workspace-policy \"can only tighten\" test came from slice 6 and feeds `allowExternalSymlinks: true` and `allowedPaths: [\".git\"]`, which have no effect.\n- **Test added:** the policy no longer has an `allowExternalSymlinks` key. It failed before the removal and passes after.\n- **Totals:** `security.test.ts` plus `security_gates.test.ts` gives 24 tests, 23 pass and 1 fail on both Node 22 and 24. The failure is the same Rust-core one. The build is clean.\n\n## Reference coding\nXERJ was active, but only the opencode corpus is peer code, and it was not verified complete.\n- **Borrowed:** the `path.relative` containment idiom (reject `..` and absolute results) from opencode.\n- **Mirrored:** the existing `verificationCandidates`, which already matches workspace-relative paths against both the raw and resolved roots.\n- **Rejected:** refusing every symlink that leaves the workspace inside `isPathProtected`, because that is a behaviour change and the Rust jail already does it.\n- **Not found:** no mature-agent coverage of protected-path matching specifically (no Codex, Claude Code, OpenHands or Aider corpora indexed).\n\n## Not verified\n- I did not rebuild the Rust core, so the pre-existing failure's cause is unconfirmed.\n- I did not run the full `make test`.\n\n## Proposed CHANGELOG entry\nFixed: protected-path matching (`.git`, `.env*`, `id_rsa*` and any `protectedPaths` from `.nexus-security.json`) is now relative to the workspace root, so projects located under directories like `~/.env-tools/` are no longer blocked wholesale. Symlinks to protected targets remain refused. Removed the unread `allowExternalSymlinks` policy key, so `.nexus-security.json` files that set it still load and the key is ignored.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>90959</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>855681</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
