{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-15T20:11:06.174Z","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a98715b8bf34d44a7</task-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68/tasks/a98715b8bf34d44a7.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Hardening slice 2: confine write-mode sandbox\" is waiting on its own background work</summary>\n<note>This agent stopped with background work of its own still running. It may resume on its own when that work completes or reports, and the same task-id notifies again if it does; the result below may be interim.</note>\n<result>**Slice 2 is committed locally on `v1/hardening-s2-sandbox-confine`, head `a0a49daa`, on top of S1 `954c601a`. It is not pushed, tagged or merged.**\n\n**Files changed**\n- `cli/src/sandbox.ts`: HOME masking, allowlist builder, network opt-in, new bwrap flags.\n- `cli/src/agent.ts`: network note in the approval prompt.\n- `cli/src/doctor.ts`: new `sandbox.confinement` check listing the effective mounts.\n- `cli/src/config.ts`, `index.ts`, `sidecar.ts`: `--sandbox-network` flag.\n- `cli/test/sandbox_confine.test.ts`: new, 9 tests.\n\n**What I changed**\n1. **HOME masking.** `--tmpfs $HOME` in write mode, then read-only bind-backs for `~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under HOME, and the operator list `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY` (read with `operatorSetting`). A `bin` entry on `PATH` also binds its parent prefix.\n   - Credential locations (`.ssh`, `.gnupg`, `.aws`, `.config/nexus`, `.config/gh`, `.netrc`, `.docker`, `.kube`, browser profiles and similar) are refused as binds, including through symlinks.\n   - I added `~/.cargo/credentials` and `credentials.toml`, which sit inside the otherwise-bound `~/.cargo`, hidden over `/dev/null`.\n   - Beyond the spec, I also mask `/run/user/&lt;uid&gt;` (agent sockets) and the docker socket.\n2. **Network.** `--unshare-net` by default. Opt in with `--sandbox-network` (all three commands) or operator-only `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1`. `--yes` does not enable it, and read-only mode always stays offline. In review mode the approval prompt says either \"runs WITH network access\" or, when the command looks like it needs the network (matched against a list of common network tools), \"will fail unless restarted with `--sandbox-network`\". I did not add a separate approval to turn the network on; it is an operator startup choice.\n3. **`--die-with-parent` and `--new-session`** are on both bwrap branches.\n4. **`--unshare-pid` and a fresh `/proc`** were already present in both branches on the base.\n5. The workspace bind is still writable, and there was no `.git`-specific handling in `sandbox.ts` to preserve.\n\n**Did each defect reproduce on the base?** I ran the new test file against the base (S1 head plus stub exports) with the throwaway HOME under `/var/tmp`.\n- **HOME readable:** yes. The sentinel `~/.ssh/id_test` was readable.\n- **Network open:** yes, no `--unshare-net` in the argv.\n- **Background child survives:** yes, it outlived the killed bwrap.\n- **`--die-with-parent`:** absent from the base argv, although `ARCHITECTURE.md` claims it.\n- **Parent-environ read: did not reproduce through bwrap.** The base already has `--unshare-pid` plus a fresh `/proc`, and the new test passes there too. The reviewer's probe most likely hit an unsandboxed `run_command`, which this slice cannot fix.\n- **One caveat:** bwrap's own pid 1 keeps the environment of whoever launched it, so `/proc/1/environ` leaks it. That is harmless in the product, because harness-core and the spawn fallback hand bwrap the scrubbed environment, but any caller that spawns bwrap with a full environment would leak it.\n- **Test setup trap:** a throwaway HOME under `/tmp` hides the leak on the base, because the sandbox puts its own tmpfs on `/tmp`.\n\n**Tests**\n- New file: 7 of 9 failed on the base, 9 of 9 pass after. The two that passed on the base are the parent-environ test and the TTY test, so neither proves a fix.\n- Related files (47 files: sandbox, evaluator, `eval_*`, `code_evaluators*`, doctor, config, read-only, worktree and `command_env` tests): **196 of 196 pass on Node 22.22.2 and on Node 24.21.0.** I did not run the base on these files, because nothing failed.\n- `npm --prefix cli run build` is clean.\n- Rust is unchanged, so I did not run `cargo test` or `clippy`. I built harness-core into the worktree-local target only to test end to end.\n- `scripts/terminal-acceptance/test-scope-procs.sh` passes. `run-cell.sh` is an interactive manual matrix, waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20. It needs an installed RC and does not use `--sandbox`, so I did not run it.\n- This was not the full `npm test` suite, only the affected files.\n\n**PTY and `--new-session`**\n- I ran sandboxed commands through harness-core in both pipe and PTY mode: output and exit codes work.\n- A timeout kill leaves no surviving children in either mode.\n- **What breaks:** under `--new-session` a command that opens `/dev/tty` directly (`sudo`, password prompts) fails with \"No such device\". Without the flag it works. Plain stdin and stdout on the PTY are unaffected.\n- TIOCSTI failed in my probe both with and without the flag, so on this kernel I could not show it being blocked. I kept the flag on both branches.\n\n**Could not verify**\n- Network reachability with the opt-in on. This host has no route to 1.1.1.1:53, so that assertion is skipped and the test prints that. The opt-in is tested only through the absence of `--unshare-net`.\n- `git`: `~/.gitconfig` is not bound (credential helpers and tokens can live there), so commits inside the sandbox lack the operator's identity.\n- The docker engine branch only got the `--network none` default change.\n- I did not update `ARCHITECTURE.md` or `USER_MANUAL.md`; they are hot files, and that is a follow-up.\n\n**Reference coding.** XERJ was active. I searched the `goose` and `opencode` prefixes for \"bwrap bubblewrap sandbox unshare\" and got only unrelated hits (a GUI iframe `sandbox` attribute and workspace \"sandboxes\" paths). The other corpora (`nexus-*`) are our own code. Nothing was borrowed from them. The design follows the repo's own read-only bwrap branch and general knowledge of Codex-style defaults. I rejected binding all of HOME read-only.\n\n**Behaviour changes and proposed CHANGELOG entry**\n- `--sandbox` commands lose network access by default.\n- `$HOME` is an empty tmpfs inside the sandbox. Writes there are lost, `~/.gitconfig`, `~/.npmrc` and `~/.ssh` are invisible, and only the listed toolchain directories are readable.\n- Sandboxed commands run in a new session with no controlling tty (no `/dev/tty`), and die with the runner.\n- Host processes are invisible inside the sandbox (this was already so before this slice).\n\n&gt; **Security: `--sandbox` now actually confines.** Write-mode sandboxed commands run with `$HOME` masked, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg` and `~/.config/nexus` are unreadable. Only toolchain directories (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under HOME) are mounted read-only; extend them with `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY`. **The network is off by default**; opt in with `--sandbox-network` or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` (not implied by `--yes`). Commands now start in a new session (no `/dev/tty`) and die with the runner. `nexus doctor` lists the effective mounts.\n\nClaims for the slice and hot files are released, and `SUITE` was held during the test runs and released too. I removed the temporary base worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/hard2-base`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>176672</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>81</tool_uses><duration_ms>3582298</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
