n` is absent.\n- **New regression test:** `cli/test/sandbox_cancel.test.ts`. It uses the real `RustCoreBridge`, and a write-mode `--sandbox` bridge for the first test. Each test runs 5 rounds of cancel and 5 rounds of deadline, with a backgrounded `sleep` in write mode. Survivors are bwrap pids that exist after the run and did not before.\n- **Test results:** `readonly_home` ran 5 times on each of Node 22.22.2 and 24.21.0, 5/5 on all ten runs, with no bwrap leaked afterwards. `sandbox_cancel` passes on the fixed code (7.7 s for write mode, 12 s for read-only).\n- **Fail-before:** with `--new-session` put back, the read-only cancel test hangs to the 170 s watchdog and leaves one bwrap, which I killed by pid. The write-mode test passed in that variant (7.9 s), so it does not catch the flag; the read-only test is the discriminating one.\n- **Wider run:** 49 related files, including `sandbox_confine`, `sandbox_quoting`, `command_env`, `process_bounds_and_services` and the new test, give 715 pass, 21 fail, 9 skipped on both Node versions.\n- **The 21 failures** are all in six eval files: `eval_dataset` (1), `eval_hardened` (4), `eval_harness` (6), `eval_integrity` (7), `eval_t3` (2) and `eval_version_probe_storage` (1). On the S1 base (`954c601a`) those same six files fail the same 21: 55 pass and 21 fail of 76 on Node 22, and the same split on Node 24. They need the CI capped-workspace fixture and similar, so they are not a regression.\n- **Earlier numbers:** my previous \"Node 22: 200 of 200\" report counted differently; I did not resolve why those eval files passed in that run, and I did not re-run them to find out.\n- **Build:** `npm --prefix cli run build` is clean on both Node versions.\n- **Leaks:** `pgrep -x bwrap` counts 0 before and 0 after the wider run. Two orphaned bwrap processes from my earlier hung `readonly_home` runs had been left behind. I killed them by exact pid (they ignored SIGTERM, so `kill -9`) and confirmed none were left. I stopped the throwaway base worktree and removed it by path.\n\n**Proposed CHANGELOG entry, updated:**\n\n&gt; **Security: `--sandbox` now actually confines.** Write-mode sandboxed commands run with `$HOME` masked, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg` and `~/.config/nexus` are unreadable. This holds even when the workspace is, contains, or sits above `$HOME`. Only toolchain directories (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under HOME) are mounted read-only; extend them with `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY`. **The network is off by default**; opt in with `--sandbox-network` or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` (not implied by `--yes`). Sandboxed commands die with the runner and cannot see host processes. `nexus doctor` lists the effective mounts and overlays.\n\nThere is no `--new-session` entry now, and the earlier note about `/dev/tty` is dropped. Claims for the slice, `sandbox.ts` and `SUITE` are released.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>227838</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>124</tool_uses><duration_ms>12626719</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
