{"parentUuid":"3fbc6c68-4d68-4214-8801-e7729365b354","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch5XabGmEwpApatfjxyNW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All seven branches are on origin at the validated heads. Now the PRs, created in dependency order so each dependent can name its base's real PR number. The three independent slices go first."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2606,"cache_read_input_tokens":893246,"output_tokens":3357,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2606,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3357,"cache_read_input_tokens":893246,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2606},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch5Xaak1FKB2shGQfLBo4","requestRef":"ded7afc4-9b99-413f-b3f6-5de0385769b0","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"878b1fb1-10ab-49f1-bc6f-7d6fe24b080c","timestamp":"2026-11-15T22:47:44.618Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"878b1fb1-10ab-49f1-bc6f-7d6fe24b080c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch5XabGmEwpApatfjxyNW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016YqHz8YJgHTry4GjL4sNt7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"FOOT='🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)'; mk() { local head=\"$1\" title=\"$2\" body=\"$3\"; gh pr create --base main --head \"$head\" --title \"$title\" --body \"$body\" >/dev/null 2>&1; gh pr list --head \"$head\" --json number -q '.[0].number'; }\nB1=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`run_command\\` inherited the Nexus process environment, which holds the operator's provider keys (\\`config.env\\` is loaded into \\`process.env\\` with override). Reproduced on main: with a sentinel \\`REDACTED in a temp HOME's \\`config.env\\`, \\`run_command \\\"env\\\"\\` printed it unsandboxed, under \\`--sandbox\\` with bwrap, and on the spawnSync fallback.\n\nNew \\`cli/src/command_env.ts\\` (\\`buildCommandEnvironment\\`, \\`isCredentialName\\`) is the one place that decides what a command sees: it drops every name that came from \\`config.env\\` (except PATH, HOME, USER, SHELL, TERM, LANG, TMPDIR, PWD, LC_*), credential-looking names (\\`*_API_KEY\\`, \\`*_TOKEN\\`, \\`*_SECRET\\`, \\`*_PASSWORD\\`, \\`AWS_*\\`, \\`GH_TOKEN\\`, \\`SSH_AUTH_SOCK\\`, \\`KUBECONFIG\\`, ...) and proxy URLs with embedded credentials. \\`NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH\\` is the operator-only opt-in (operator environment or \\`config.env\\`, never a project \\`.env\\`). harness-core and the spawnSync fallback both use it; the write-mode bwrap branch uses \\`--clearenv\\` plus the scrubbed variables; \\`core/src/runner.rs\\` strips the same names as a second layer.\n\n**Known limit, stated in the docs PR:** without \\`--sandbox\\` a command runs as the same user, so it can still read the operator's files and its parents' \\`/proc/<pid>/environ\\`. This PR removes what a command inherits, not what the user's own processes can read.\n\n## Validation\n\nReproduced before the fix (3 of 3 modes leaked). New \\`command_env.test.ts\\`: 4 of 4; with \\`sandbox_quoting\\` and \\`readonly_home\\`, 11 of 11 on Node 22 and 24 (bwrap present, nothing skipped), re-run independently. \\`cargo test\\` and \\`cargo clippy -D warnings\\` clean per the implementing agent.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ was active but its corpora returned nothing relevant to environment scrubbing. Took the shape of the repo's own \\`buildMcpEnvironment\\`/\\`buildVerifierEnvironment\\`/\\`buildLspEnvironment\\`; Codex's \\`shell_environment_policy\\` idea is from memory, not verified against source. Rejected an allow list (agent commands need an ordinary toolchain environment).\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\nCommands that relied on \\`SSH_AUTH_SOCK\\`, \\`GH_TOKEN\\` and similar must opt in via \\`NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH\\`. Changelog entry is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7).\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] Behaviour is documented in the docs PR (hardening 3/7), which merges last in its chain.\n\n$FOOT\"\nP1=$(mk v1/hardening-s1-command-env \"fix(security): keep operator credentials out of run_command environments (hardening 1/7)\" \"$B1\"); echo \"S1 -> #$P1\"\nB4=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree\\` ran plain \\`git status\\`, \\`git add -A\\` and \\`git commit\\` inside the agent-writable task worktree with no hardening. **Reproduced on main:** replacing the worktree's \\`.git\\` with a directory holding a \\`config\\` with \\`core.fsmonitor\\` or \\`core.hooksPath\\` plus a \\`pre-commit\\` hook made planted code run outside the worktree (sentinel files appeared). No existing check stopped it.\n\n\\`mergeWorktree\\` now refuses a worktree that is not a verified linked worktree of this repository before any Git call, and every later call goes through \\`worktrunk/git.ts\\` (pinned \\`--git-dir\\`/\\`--work-tree\\`, \\`core.hooksPath=/dev/null\\`, \\`core.fsmonitor=false\\`, the Git 2.54+ hook switches, scrubbed environment, no prompts, \\`--end-of-options\\` on refs). \\`computeGitCandidateIdentity\\` also refuses an untrusted checkout and pins fsmonitor and hooks off.\n\n## Validation\n\nNew \\`worktree_merge_planted_git.test.ts\\` (4 tests). **Run against unfixed main, all 4 fail** (including 'a legitimate merge fires no hook', because main fires repository hooks); on this branch 118 of 118 across it and four neighbouring files, on Node 22 and 24, re-run independently.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ (codex, opencode corpora): took Codex's per-invocation \\`core.hooksPath=/dev/null\\` and fsmonitor override and the shape of its 'does not execute configured hooks, fsmonitor or filters' sentinel test; rejected Codex's fsmonitor-detection probe (we pin it off) and opencode's per-repo gitconfig (trusts the repository's own config).\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\n**Behaviour change:** the operator's own git hooks (pre-commit, commit-msg, post-commit, post-merge) no longer fire on Nexus's legacy task-worktree commit and merge, matching the \\`worktrunk\\` path; a tampered worktree \\`.git\\` is refused with an error.\n**Follow-ups, not done here:** routing this path through the \\`worktrunk\\` merge implementation (larger change); \\`evaluator.ts:210-236\\` runs \\`git diff\\` in \\`evalWorkspace\\` without the guard (\\`git apply\\` runs no hooks; whether that workspace is agent-writable was not checked). Other git call sites were grepped: guarded ones listed in the commit message; read-only \\`rev-parse\\` calls left.\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] User-visible text is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7).\n\n$FOOT\"\nP4=$(mk v1/hardening-s4-worktree-merge \"fix(cli): legacy worktree merge runs no code from an agent-planted .git (hardening 4/7)\" \"$B4\"); echo \"S4 -> #$P4\"\nB6=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`SecurityManager.isPathProtected\\` checked every segment of the absolute and resolved paths, so a workspace under \\`~/.env-tools/\\`, \\`~/id_rsa-backup/\\` or \\`.git/\\` had every file reported protected. **Reproduced on main** for all three layouts. Patterns now match the path relative to the workspace root (and relative to the resolved root for the symlink check). A symlink that resolves outside the workspace is matched on its absolute path, so a link to a protected target is still refused; absolute policy patterns still match the absolute path; both the real and lexical workspace roots are tried (a workspace behind a symlink such as \\`/tmp\\`).\n\n## Validation\n\n7 new tests in \\`security.test.ts\\` (the three layouts; protected names in an ordinary workspace; \\`.git/hooks/x\\`, \\`sub/.env.local\\`, \\`id_rsa\\`; a symlink to \\`.git\\`; a symlink resolving outside to a protected target; a workspace policy can only add protections). 3 of them fail before the fix. With the Rust core built, **\\`security.test.ts\\` plus \\`security_gates.test.ts\\` pass 23 of 23 on Node 22 and 24** (re-run independently; the one failure the implementing agent saw was the unbuilt core, and it also exercises the end-to-end refusal).\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ's only peer corpus here (opencode) is not verified complete. Borrowed the \\`path.relative\\` containment idiom; mirrored the existing \\`verificationCandidates\\`; rejected refusing every outward symlink inside \\`isPathProtected\\` (a behaviour change; the Rust jail already does it). No mature-agent coverage of protected-path matching was found.\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\nFixed: protected-path matching is workspace-relative, so projects under directories such as \\`~/.env-tools/\\` are no longer blocked wholesale; symlinks to protected targets remain refused. Changelog entry is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7). Slice 7 (#PLACEHOLDER7) is stacked on this one.\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] Covered by the docs PR.\n\n$FOOT\"\nP6=$(mk v1/hardening-s6-protected-paths \"fix(security): match protected paths relative to the workspace root (hardening 6/7)\" \"$B6\"); echo \"S6 -> #$P6\"; echo \"$P1 $P4 $P6\" > /tmp/claude-1000/pr_nums_a.txt","description":"Open the three independent hardening PRs (slices 1, 4, 6) and capture their numbers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2606,"cache_read_input_tokens":893246,"output_tokens":3357,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2606,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3357,"cache_read_input_tokens":893246,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2606,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2606},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":18839,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016YqHz8YJgHTry4GjL4sNt7":{"command":"cd /home/mike/nexus-code && FOOT='🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)'; mk() { local head=\"$1\" title=\"$2\" body=\"$3\"; gh pr create --base main --head \"$head\" --title \"$title\" --body \"$body\" >/dev/null 2>&1; gh pr list --head \"$head\" --json number -q '.[0].number'; }\nB1=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`run_command\\` inherited the Nexus process environment, which holds the operator's provider keys (\\`config.env\\` is loaded into \\`process.env\\` with override). Reproduced on main: with a sentinel \\`REDACTED in a temp HOME's \\`config.env\\`, \\`run_command \\\"env\\\"\\` printed it unsandboxed, under \\`--sandbox\\` with bwrap, and on the spawnSync fallback.\n\nNew \\`cli/src/command_env.ts\\` (\\`buildCommandEnvironment\\`, \\`isCredentialName\\`) is the one place that decides what a command sees: it drops every name that came from \\`config.env\\` (except PATH, HOME, USER, SHELL, TERM, LANG, TMPDIR, PWD, LC_*), credential-looking names (\\`*_API_KEY\\`, \\`*_TOKEN\\`, \\`*_SECRET\\`, \\`*_PASSWORD\\`, \\`AWS_*\\`, \\`GH_TOKEN\\`, \\`SSH_AUTH_SOCK\\`, \\`KUBECONFIG\\`, ...) and proxy URLs with embedded credentials. \\`NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH\\` is the operator-only opt-in (operator environment or \\`config.env\\`, never a project \\`.env\\`). harness-core and the spawnSync fallback both use it; the write-mode bwrap branch uses \\`--clearenv\\` plus the scrubbed variables; \\`core/src/runner.rs\\` strips the same names as a second layer.\n\n**Known limit, stated in the docs PR:** without \\`--sandbox\\` a command runs as the same user, so it can still read the operator's files and its parents' \\`/proc/<pid>/environ\\`. This PR removes what a command inherits, not what the user's own processes can read.\n\n## Validation\n\nReproduced before the fix (3 of 3 modes leaked). New \\`command_env.test.ts\\`: 4 of 4; with \\`sandbox_quoting\\` and \\`readonly_home\\`, 11 of 11 on Node 22 and 24 (bwrap present, nothing skipped), re-run independently. \\`cargo test\\` and \\`cargo clippy -D warnings\\` clean per the implementing agent.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ was active but its corpora returned nothing relevant to environment scrubbing. Took the shape of the repo's own \\`buildMcpEnvironment\\`/\\`buildVerifierEnvironment\\`/\\`buildLspEnvironment\\`; Codex's \\`shell_environment_policy\\` idea is from memory, not verified against source. Rejected an allow list (agent commands need an ordinary toolchain environment).\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\nCommands that relied on \\`SSH_AUTH_SOCK\\`, \\`GH_TOKEN\\` and similar must opt in via \\`NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH\\`. Changelog entry is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7).\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] Behaviour is documented in the docs PR (hardening 3/7), which merges last in its chain.\n\n$FOOT\"\nP1=$(mk v1/hardening-s1-command-env \"fix(security): keep operator credentials out of run_command environments (hardening 1/7)\" \"$B1\"); echo \"S1 -> #$P1\"\nB4=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`WorktreeManager.mergeWorktree\\` ran plain \\`git status\\`, \\`git add -A\\` and \\`git commit\\` inside the agent-writable task worktree with no hardening. **Reproduced on main:** replacing the worktree's \\`.git\\` with a directory holding a \\`config\\` with \\`core.fsmonitor\\` or \\`core.hooksPath\\` plus a \\`pre-commit\\` hook made planted code run outside the worktree (sentinel files appeared). No existing check stopped it.\n\n\\`mergeWorktree\\` now refuses a worktree that is not a verified linked worktree of this repository before any Git call, and every later call goes through \\`worktrunk/git.ts\\` (pinned \\`--git-dir\\`/\\`--work-tree\\`, \\`core.hooksPath=/dev/null\\`, \\`core.fsmonitor=false\\`, the Git 2.54+ hook switches, scrubbed environment, no prompts, \\`--end-of-options\\` on refs). \\`computeGitCandidateIdentity\\` also refuses an untrusted checkout and pins fsmonitor and hooks off.\n\n## Validation\n\nNew \\`worktree_merge_planted_git.test.ts\\` (4 tests). **Run against unfixed main, all 4 fail** (including 'a legitimate merge fires no hook', because main fires repository hooks); on this branch 118 of 118 across it and four neighbouring files, on Node 22 and 24, re-run independently.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ (codex, opencode corpora): took Codex's per-invocation \\`core.hooksPath=/dev/null\\` and fsmonitor override and the shape of its 'does not execute configured hooks, fsmonitor or filters' sentinel test; rejected Codex's fsmonitor-detection probe (we pin it off) and opencode's per-repo gitconfig (trusts the repository's own config).\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\n**Behaviour change:** the operator's own git hooks (pre-commit, commit-msg, post-commit, post-merge) no longer fire on Nexus's legacy task-worktree commit and merge, matching the \\`worktrunk\\` path; a tampered worktree \\`.git\\` is refused with an error.\n**Follow-ups, not done here:** routing this path through the \\`worktrunk\\` merge implementation (larger change); \\`evaluator.ts:210-236\\` runs \\`git diff\\` in \\`evalWorkspace\\` without the guard (\\`git apply\\` runs no hooks; whether that workspace is agent-writable was not checked). Other git call sites were grepped: guarded ones listed in the commit message; read-only \\`rev-parse\\` calls left.\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] User-visible text is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7).\n\n$FOOT\"\nP4=$(mk v1/hardening-s4-worktree-merge \"fix(cli): legacy worktree merge runs no code from an agent-planted .git (hardening 4/7)\" \"$B4\"); echo \"S4 -> #$P4\"\nB6=\"## Problem and resulting behavior\n\n\\`SecurityManager.isPathProtected\\` checked every segment of the absolute and resolved paths, so a workspace under \\`~/.env-tools/\\`, \\`~/id_rsa-backup/\\` or \\`.git/\\` had every file reported protected. **Reproduced on main** for all three layouts. Patterns now match the path relative to the workspace root (and relative to the resolved root for the symlink check). A symlink that resolves outside the workspace is matched on its absolute path, so a link to a protected target is still refused; absolute policy patterns still match the absolute path; both the real and lexical workspace roots are tried (a workspace behind a symlink such as \\`/tmp\\`).\n\n## Validation\n\n7 new tests in \\`security.test.ts\\` (the three layouts; protected names in an ordinary workspace; \\`.git/hooks/x\\`, \\`sub/.env.local\\`, \\`id_rsa\\`; a symlink to \\`.git\\`; a symlink resolving outside to a protected target; a workspace policy can only add protections). 3 of them fail before the fix. With the Rust core built, **\\`security.test.ts\\` plus \\`security_gates.test.ts\\` pass 23 of 23 on Node 22 and 24** (re-run independently; the one failure the implementing agent saw was the unbuilt core, and it also exercises the end-to-end refusal).\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ's only peer corpus here (opencode) is not verified complete. Borrowed the \\`path.relative\\` containment idiom; mirrored the existing \\`verificationCandidates\\`; rejected refusing every outward symlink inside \\`isPathProtected\\` (a behaviour change; the Rust jail already does it). No mature-agent coverage of protected-path matching was found.\n\n## Compatibility, security, and recovery\n\nFixed: protected-path matching is workspace-relative, so projects under directories such as \\`~/.env-tools/\\` are no longer blocked wholesale; symlinks to protected targets remain refused. Changelog entry is in the docs PR (hardening 3/7). Slice 7 (#PLACEHOLDER7) is stacked on this one.\n\n## Documentation and coordination\n\n- [x] Covered by the docs PR.\n\n$FOOT\"\nP6=$(mk v1/hardening-s6-protected-paths \"fix(security): match protected paths relative to the workspace root (hardening 6/7)\" \"$B6\"); echo \"S6 -> #$P6\"; echo \"$P1 $P4 $P6\" > /tmp/claude-1000/pr_nums_a.txt","description":"Open the three independent hardening PRs (slices 1, 4, 6) and capture their numbers"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_016YqHz8YJgHTry4GjL4sNt7":{"cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch5Xaak1FKB2shGQfLBo4","requestRef":"ded7afc4-9b99-413f-b3f6-5de0385769b0","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"17c8eadf-eedb-4812-adfb-f6939b1f6851","timestamp":"2026-11-15T22:48:03.493Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
