{"parentUuid":"115f559b-4225-4739-8b62-fcb84ebb4a7c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01B8Br1xsFAeSzEQVg4epBja","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"claude-mem hook claude-code file-context","durationMs":487},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f9516f9d-cce9-422c-a094-2723d4f11af8","timestamp":"2026-11-16T03:10:44.189Z","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"f9516f9d-cce9-422c-a094-2723d4f11af8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["graphify: knowledge graph at graphify-out/. For focused questions, run `graphify query \"<question>\"` (scoped subgraph, usually much smaller than GRAPH_REPORT.md) instead of grepping raw files. Read GRAPH_REPORT.md only for broad architecture context."],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01B8Br1xsFAeSzEQVg4epBja","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"23c7f198-320b-4d64-a1b5-741368264902","timestamp":"2026-11-16T03:10:44.253Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Bash hook additional context: graphify: knowledge graph at graphify-out/. For focused questions, run `graphify query \"<question>\"` (scoped subgraph, usually much smaller than GRAPH_REPORT.md) instead of grepping raw files. Read GRAPH_REPORT.md only for broad architecture context.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
{"parentUuid":"f2006428-f558-4897-84df-1374b8bf883c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1df71b39-70bc-48d8-8601-54acd84aba87","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B8Br1xsFAeSzEQVg4epBja","type":"tool_result","content":"9:## [Unreleased]\n10-\n11-### Security\n12-\n13-- Commands run through `run_command` no longer inherit credentials from the Nexus process. Variables from `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, plus anything named like a key, token, secret or password, `AWS_*`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar, are removed from the command environment, including inside the write-mode bwrap sandbox. To pass a variable through deliberately, list its name in `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (operator environment or `config.env` only). Commands that relied on `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar must now opt in. Without `--sandbox` a command still runs as your user and can read your files and its parent processes' environment.\n14-- `--sandbox` now actually confines. Write-mode sandboxed commands run with `$HOME` masked, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg` and `~/.config/nexus` are unreadable, even when the workspace is, contains, or sits above `$HOME`. Only toolchain directories (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under HOME) are mounted read-only; extend them with `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY`. The network is off by default; opt in with `--sandbox-network` or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` (not implied by `--yes`). Sandboxed commands die with the runner and cannot see host processes. `nexus doctor` lists the effective mounts and overlays. Files outside `$HOME` remain readable inside the sandbox.\n15-- The legacy task-worktree merge refuses a worktree whose `.git` was replaced, and runs Git with hooks and fsmonitor disabled; a planted `core.fsmonitor` or hook in an agent-writable worktree no longer runs code outside it. The repository's own hooks no longer fire on that commit and merge.\n16-- The `--sandbox docker` engine runs a digest-pinned image (alpine 3.23) as the invoking uid with all capabilities dropped, `no-new-privileges`, a read-only root plus a tmpfs `/tmp`, a 512-process limit, and no network unless `--sandbox-network` / `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` is set. Only the scrubbed command environment reaches the container, and credential locations under a workspace that is or contains `$HOME` are masked. `NEXUS_SANDBOX_DOCKER_IMAGE` (operator setting) may substitute another image but it must be digest-pinned, otherwise no container is started. Commands that wrote outside the workspace and `/tmp`, relied on root, or used the network now fail in Docker mode.\n17-\n18-### Fixed\n19-\n20-- Protected-path matching is relative to the workspace root, so projects under directories such as `~/.env-tools/` are no longer blocked wholesale; symlinks to protected targets are still refused.\n21-\n22-### Removed\n23-\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4103781b-d517-4572-9c99-a00ba241badc","timestamp":"2026-11-16T03:10:44.439Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"9:## [Unreleased]\n10-\n11-### Security\n12-\n13-- Commands run through `run_command` no longer inherit credentials from the Nexus process. Variables from `~/.config/nexus/config.env`, plus anything named like a key, token, secret or password, `AWS_*`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar, are removed from the command environment, including inside the write-mode bwrap sandbox. To pass a variable through deliberately, list its name in `NEXUS_COMMAND_ENV_PASSTHROUGH` (operator environment or `config.env` only). Commands that relied on `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, `GH_TOKEN` and similar must now opt in. Without `--sandbox` a command still runs as your user and can read your files and its parent processes' environment.\n14-- `--sandbox` now actually confines. Write-mode sandboxed commands run with `$HOME` masked, so `~/.ssh`, `~/.aws`, `~/.gnupg` and `~/.config/nexus` are unreadable, even when the workspace is, contains, or sits above `$HOME`. Only toolchain directories (`~/.cargo`, `~/.rustup`, `~/.nvm`, `~/.local/bin`, `~/.npm`, `~/.cache/pip`, `~/go/pkg`, `PATH` entries under HOME) are mounted read-only; extend them with `NEXUS_SANDBOX_HOME_READONLY`. The network is off by default; opt in with `--sandbox-network` or `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` (not implied by `--yes`). Sandboxed commands die with the runner and cannot see host processes. `nexus doctor` lists the effective mounts and overlays. Files outside `$HOME` remain readable inside the sandbox.\n15-- The legacy task-worktree merge refuses a worktree whose `.git` was replaced, and runs Git with hooks and fsmonitor disabled; a planted `core.fsmonitor` or hook in an agent-writable worktree no longer runs code outside it. The repository's own hooks no longer fire on that commit and merge.\n16-- The `--sandbox docker` engine runs a digest-pinned image (alpine 3.23) as the invoking uid with all capabilities dropped, `no-new-privileges`, a read-only root plus a tmpfs `/tmp`, a 512-process limit, and no network unless `--sandbox-network` / `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK=1` is set. Only the scrubbed command environment reaches the container, and credential locations under a workspace that is or contains `$HOME` are masked. `NEXUS_SANDBOX_DOCKER_IMAGE` (operator setting) may substitute another image but it must be digest-pinned, otherwise no container is started. Commands that wrote outside the workspace and `/tmp`, relied on root, or used the network now fail in Docker mode.\n17-\n18-### Fixed\n19-\n20-- Protected-path matching is relative to the workspace root, so projects under directories such as `~/.env-tools/` are no longer blocked wholesale; symlinks to protected targets are still refused.\n21-\n22-### Removed\n23-","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f2006428-f558-4897-84df-1374b8bf883c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"6473a60e-9fa2-4bd9-9d0c-35324295cf68","version":"2.1.323","gitBranch":"main","slug":"robust-rolling-otter"}
